QB.add(17,[{"id":17001,"t":"Body Cavities and Their Linings","f":"bc07","s":"Which pairing of body cavity and its lining is correct?","o":["Cranial – meninges; thoracic – pleura and pericardium; abdominal – peritoneum","Cranial – meninges; thoracic – peritoneum and pericardium; abdominal – pleura","Cranial – pericardium; thoracic – pleura and meninges; abdominal – peritoneum","Cranial – meninges; thoracic – pleura only; abdominal – pericardium","All three cavities – meninges"],"e":"The body keeps its <b>internal organisation</b> using membranes, sheaths and structures that separate compartments. The <b>cranial cavity</b> is lined by the <b>meninges</b>; the <b>thoracic</b> and <b>abdominal</b> cavities are each lined by a <b>serous membrane</b>: <b>pleura</b> (lungs), <b>pericardium</b> (heart), <b>peritoneum</b> (abdominal walls reflecting onto organs). Memory aid: M-P-P-P.","w":["Thoracic: pleura, pericardium.","Cranial: meninges.","Pericardium is thoracic.","Different linings."],"k":"Meninges (cranial); serous membranes (thorax, abdomen).","rn":[["","What are the 4 membranes that wrap organs in the body cavities?","What are the 4 membranes that wrap organs in the body cavities?","Meninges; Pleura; Pericardium; Peritoneum"],["","What are the 3 serous membranes?","What are the 3 serous membranes?","Pleura; Pericardium; Peritoneum"],["","Which membranes are in the thoracic cavity?","Which membranes are in the thoracic cavity?","Pleura; Pericardium"]]},{"id":17002,"t":"Cranial Cavity","s":"Which ordering of the meninges from outside to inside is correct?","o":["Dura → arachnoid → pia","Pia → arachnoid → dura","Arachnoid → dura → pia","Dura → pia → arachnoid","Pia → dura → arachnoid"],"e":"Three membranous layers, <b>outside → inside</b>: <b>dura</b> (tough), <b>arachnoid</b> (spider-web), <b>pia</b> (smooth, closely adherent). They continue down around the spinal cord. Memory aid: “Don't Annoy Pia”.","w":["Reversed.","Dura outermost.","Arachnoid middle.","Wrong."],"k":"D-A-P outside → in.","rn":[["","What are the 3 layers of the meninges, outside to inside?","What are the 3 layers of the meninges, outside to inside?","Dura mater; Arachnoid mater; Pia mater; MNEMONIC"],["","What are the 3 meningeal layers around the spinal cord?","What are the 3 meningeal layers around the spinal cord?","Dura mater; Arachnoid mater; Pia mater"],["","What is the order of layers from bone to brain?","What is the order of layers from bone to brain?","Dura; Arachnoid; Subarachnoid space; Pia"]]},{"id":17003,"t":"Cranial Cavity","f":"bc10","s":"In the cranial cavity, what are the two layers of the dura mater, and what lies between them?","o":["Periosteal and meningeal layers, with venous sinuses between","Periosteal and meningeal layers, with CSF between","Arachnoid and pia layers, with CSF between","Visceral and parietal layers, with serous fluid between","Fibrous and serous layers, with venous sinuses between"],"e":"Cranial dura: <b>outer periosteal (endosteal)</b> layer adhered to bone, and <b>inner meningeal</b> layer. Between them is a blood-filled space – a <b>venous sinus</b> – which <b>functions like a vein</b> but is <b>histologically different</b>: its walls are the <b>two layers of dura</b>.","w":["Blood.","Not dura.","Serous.","Not dura."],"k":"Cranial dura: periosteal + meningeal; sinuses between.","rn":[["","What are the 2 layers of the dura mater in the cranial cavity?","What are the 2 layers of the dura mater in the cranial cavity?","Outer periosteal (endosteal) layer; Inner meningeal layer"],["","Periosteal vs meningeal layer of dura: position","Periosteal vs meningeal layer of dura: position","Periosteal: outer, on bone; Meningeal: inner"]]},{"id":17004,"t":"Cranial Cavity","s":"What is the principal difference between a dural venous sinus and an ordinary vein?","o":["Its walls are formed by the two dural layers","Its walls are formed by arachnoid and pia","It carries arterial blood","It contains CSF instead of blood","It has thicker smooth muscle"],"e":"A dural sinus <b>functions like a vein</b> but its walls are made of the <b>two layers of dura mater</b> – the principal histological difference. The word “sinus” needs context: blood sinuses, air sinuses in skull bones, liver sinusoids, the coronary sinus.","w":["Dura.","Venous.","Blood.","Dura walls."],"k":"Sinus walls = two dural layers.","rn":[["","Dural sinus vs ordinary vein: main difference?","Dural sinus vs ordinary vein: main difference?","Sinus wall is; Two layers of dura"]]},{"id":17005,"t":"Cranial Cavity","s":"A blow to the side of the head causes a lens-shaped bleed between skull and dura. Name the artery most likely torn.","e":"An extradural haematoma usually comes from the middle meningeal artery at the side of the head, between periosteal dura and bone.","k":"Middle meningeal artery.","a":["middle meningeal artery","middle meningeal"],"rn":[["","Where does the middle meningeal artery run?","Where does the middle meningeal artery run?","Between periosteal (outer) dura; And cranial bones"],["","What typically causes middle meningeal artery bleeding?","What typically causes middle meningeal artery bleeding?","Direct hit: side of head"],["","Extradural Haematoma","Extradural Haematoma","Bleeding outside the dura; From the middle meningeal artery; Peels dura off the skull"]]},{"id":17006,"t":"Cranial Cavity","f":"bc12","s":"Which statement about the subarachnoid space is correct?","o":["Between arachnoid and pia, with CSF and vessels","Between dura and arachnoid, with CSF and vessels","Between dura and skull, with CSF and vessels","Between the two dural layers, with venous blood","Between pia and brain, with serous fluid"],"e":"The <b>arachnoid</b> is separated from the <b>pia</b> by the <b>subarachnoid space</b>, containing <b>CSF</b> and <b>blood vessels</b>. “Arachnoid” means spider – it looks like a web, and the space is beneath (sub) it.","w":["Potential.","Extradural.","Sinus.","Not."],"k":"Subarachnoid: CSF + vessels.","rn":[["","Subarachnoid Space","Subarachnoid Space","Space between arachnoid and pia; Contains CSF and blood vessels"]]},{"id":17007,"t":"Cranial Cavity","s":"Why did early anatomists describe only two meningeal layers?","o":["Arachnoid is attached to dura, leaving only a potential space","Arachnoid is fused to the pia, leaving no space between","Pia mater does not exist around the brain","Dura mater is absent around the spinal cord","They used only microscopes, not dissection"],"e":"The <b>arachnoid is attached to the dura</b>, so the dura–arachnoid space is only <b>potential</b>, appearing when there is <b>bleeding</b>. Early anatomists saw a tough layer (dura, “hard mother”) and a smooth layer (pia, “gentle mother”).","w":["Attached to dura.","Pia exists.","Present.","Not."],"k":"Arachnoid attached to dura → two seen.","rn":[["","What is the space between the dura and arachnoid?","What is the space between the dura and arachnoid?","Only a potential space"]]},{"id":17008,"t":"Cranial Cavity","s":"Name the innermost meningeal layer, adherent to the brain like the skin of a peeled boiled egg.","e":"Pia mater (“gentle mother”) is closely adherent to the brain and cord.","k":"Pia mater.","a":["pia mater","pia"],"rn":[["","Pia mater","Pia mater","Innermost meningeal layer; Adheres to brain, cord"],["","What are the 3 meningeal layers around the spinal cord?","What are the 3 meningeal layers around the spinal cord?","Dura mater; Arachnoid mater; Pia mater"]]},{"id":17009,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"A patient has central upper abdominal pain just below the xiphisternum. Name the region labelled <b>A</b>.","e":"A is the epigastric region, between the right (B) and left (H) hypochondria, above the subcostal plane. The nine regions use two midclavicular planes plus the subcostal and intertubercular planes.","k":"A = epigastric.","img":{"src":"17/regions","cap":"Anterior abdominal wall divided by two midclavicular, subcostal and intertubercular planes. Labels removed."},"a":["epigastric","epigastrium","epigastric region"],"rn":[["","What is the upper central region called?","What is the upper central region called?","Epigastric"],["","What are the 3 central regions of the abdomen?","What are the 3 central regions of the abdomen?","Epigastric; Umbilical; Pubic"]]},{"id":17010,"t":"Serous Membranes","f":"bc17","s":"Which definitions of serous membrane parts are correct?","o":["Visceral covers the organ; parietal lines the wall; a fluid-filled potential space","Visceral lines the wall; parietal covers the organ; a fluid-filled potential space","Visceral covers the organ; parietal lines the wall; an air-filled space","Visceral covers the organ; parietal lines the wall; the organ lies within the space","A single layer covering the organ"],"e":"<b>Visceral</b>: covers the organ's outer surface. <b>Parietal</b>: lines the inner surface of the body wall. <b>Cavity</b>: narrow <b>potential</b> space with a <b>thin film of serous fluid</b>. Named after the organ: pleural, pericardial, peritoneal cavity (“space” and “cavity” mean the same).","w":["Swapped.","Fluid.","Outside.","Two layers."],"k":"Visceral organ; parietal wall; cavity fluid.","rn":[["","Visceral vs parietal layer: what each is attached to","Visceral vs parietal layer: what each is attached to","Visceral: the organ; Parietal: the body wall"],["","What is the serous cavity?","What is the serous cavity?","Narrow potential space; Thin film of serous fluid"],["","Visceral Layer","Visceral Layer","Covers the organ's outer surface"]]},{"id":17011,"t":"Serous Membranes","f":"bc18","s":"A patient's pain begins vague and poorly localised, then becomes sharp and pinpointed as inflammation reaches the body wall lining. Which explanation fits?","o":["Visceral layer: visceral sensory (vague); parietal: somatic (sharp)","Visceral layer: somatic (sharp); parietal: visceral sensory (vague)","Both layers: somatic sensory supply","Both layers: visceral sensory supply","Pain arises from the serous fluid"],"e":"<b>Visceral</b> layer: <b>visceral sensory</b> – pain usually <b>poorly localised</b> or <b>referred</b>. <b>Parietal</b> layer: <b>somatic sensory</b> – pain <b>sharp and well localised</b> (you can point at it). This becomes important with referred pain later.","w":["Swapped.","Visceral is visceral.","Parietal is somatic.","Not."],"k":"Visceral vague; parietal sharp.","rn":[["","Visceral vs parietal layer: pain","Visceral vs parietal layer: pain","Visceral: vague, referred; Parietal: sharp, localised"],["","Visceral vs parietal layer: innervation","Visceral vs parietal layer: innervation","Visceral: visceral sensory; Parietal: somatic sensory"]]},{"id":17012,"t":"Serous Membranes","f":"bc19","s":"Why is a serous cavity described as closed with “no way out”?","o":["The visceral layer is continuous with the parietal, sealing the space","The visceral layer is separate from the parietal, sealed by fluid","The cavity is filled with solid tissue","The cavity opens to the skin surface","The organ itself lies inside the cavity"],"e":"Embryologically the organ pushes into a sac: the part against the organ becomes <b>visceral</b>, the outer part <b>parietal</b>. The visceral layer is <b>continuous</b> with the parietal layer, so the space is <b>closed</b>. The organ remains attached by a stalk of vessels and nerves (mesentery for peritoneum).","w":["Continuous.","Not.","Not.","Outside."],"k":"Continuous layers → closed cavity.","rn":[["","How do the visceral and parietal layers relate?","How do the visceral and parietal layers relate?","Each continues into the other; One continuous layer"],["","Pleural cavity","Pleural cavity","Closed space; Between visceral and parietal pleura; Contains pleural fluid"]]},{"id":17013,"t":"Cranial Cavity","f":"bc20","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> a function of serous membranes listed?","o":["Producing CSF","Reducing friction","Compartmentalising","Support","Acting as a conduit"],"e":"Serous membrane functions: <b>reduce friction</b> (fluid between layers), <b>compartmentalise</b>, <b>support</b>, <b>conduit</b>, <b>defence</b>. Memory aid: “Friendly Cats Support Clever Dogs”. CSF is in the subarachnoid space.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Listed."],"k":"Friction, compartmentalise, support, conduit, defence.","rn":[["","CSF","CSF","Cerebrospinal fluid"],["","Subarachnoid Space","Subarachnoid Space","Space between arachnoid and pia; Contains CSF and blood vessels"]]},{"id":17014,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"How does the mesentery illustrate the “conduit” and “support” functions of serous membranes?","o":["It carries vessels and nerves and suspends organs","It carries vessels, nerves and CSF between its layers and suspends organs","It lines the cranial cavity and supports the brain","It covers retroperitoneal organs on their front only","It contains air that cushions the bowel"],"e":"A <b>mesentery</b> is a <b>double layer of peritoneum</b> with <b>vessels, nerves and lymphatics</b> between its layers (conduit) and it <b>suspends</b> intraperitoneal organs (support).","w":["Lymphatics, not CSF.","Meninges.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Mesentery = conduit + support.","rn":[["","Mesentery","Mesentery","Double layer of peritoneum; Carries vessels, nerves and lymphatics"]]},{"id":17015,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"Name the central thoracic compartment between the two pleural cavities, containing the heart, oesophagus and trachea.","e":"The thoracic cavity has three compartments: right and left pleural cavities and the mediastinum.","k":"Mediastinum.","a":["mediastinum"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 compartments of the thoracic cavity?","What are the 3 compartments of the thoracic cavity?","Right pleural cavity; Left pleural cavity; Mediastinum"]]},{"id":17016,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"How many main compartments does the thoracic cavity have and what are they?","o":["Three: right pleural, left pleural, mediastinum","Two: right pleural and left pleural","Three: pleural, pericardial and peritoneal","Four quadrants","Nine regions"],"e":"Thoracic cavity: <b>right pleural cavity</b> (right lung), <b>left pleural cavity</b> (left lung) and the central <b>mediastinum</b> (pericardium with heart, trachea, oesophagus, great vessels).","w":["Mediastinum.","Peritoneal is abdominal.","Abdomen.","Abdomen."],"k":"Thorax: 2 pleural cavities + mediastinum.","rn":[["","What are the 3 compartments of the thoracic cavity?","What are the 3 compartments of the thoracic cavity?","Right pleural cavity; Left pleural cavity; Mediastinum"]]},{"id":17017,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","f":"bc23","s":"On an axial CT of the chest, the heart appears on the right side of your screen. Which side of the patient is that?","o":["The patient’s left – viewed from the feet","The patient’s right – viewed from the head","The patient’s left – viewed from the head","The patient’s right – viewed from the feet","It cannot be determined"],"e":"CT images are viewed <b>from below</b>: the patient lies supine with feet towards you, so the <b>patient's left is on your right</b>. Clue: the <b>heart tilts left</b>; in the abdomen the <b>liver</b> is on the patient's right; the vertebra marks posterior.","w":["Feet.","Left.","Left.","It can."],"k":"Axial: patient's left on your right.","rn":[["","Image card: Which side of this axial CT is the patient's left? How can you tell?","Which side of this axial CT is the patient's left? How can you tell?","Screen right; Heart tilts to the left; CT is viewed from below"],["","Image card: Which side of this axial CT is the patient's right? How can you tell?","Which side of this axial CT is the patient's right? How can you tell?","Screen left; Liver is on right; CT viewed from below"],["","From which direction do you view an axial CT?","From which direction do you view an axial CT?","From below (from the feet)"]]},{"id":17018,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Two axial sections of the thorax look different: one shows lungs, sternum, ribs, vertebra and scapula but no heart; the other shows the heart. What does this teach?","o":["Sections differ by level; use landmarks","The heart moves between the two scans","The upper section is abnormal","The scapulae lie in the mediastinum","The lungs are absent at the lower level"],"e":"An axial section looks <b>different depending on the level</b>: the upper level shows lungs, sternum, ribs, vertebra and scapula with the mediastinum between the lungs; the lower level shows the <b>heart</b>. Always use <b>landmarks</b>.","w":["Level.","Not.","Not.","They are present."],"k":"Level determines appearance.","rn":[["","Which landmarks help you find the level of an axial section?","Which landmarks help you find the level of an axial section?","Liver; Ribs; Bones (e.g. pelvis, vertebra)"]]},{"id":17019,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","f":"bc24","s":"Which statement about the pleura is correct?","o":["Continuous double layer: visceral reflects into parietal around a closed space","Continuous double layer: visceral reflects into fibrous around a closed space","Single layer lining the chest wall only","Double layer with a space opening into the mediastinum","Double layer with CSF between"],"e":"The <b>pleura</b> is a <b>continuous, double-layered</b> membrane around the lungs: <b>visceral</b> (on lung) and <b>parietal</b> (outer). The visceral pleura reflects and continues as parietal, so the <b>pleural cavity is closed</b> (“no way out, no way in”) with a thin film of <b>pleural fluid</b> to prevent friction.","w":["Parietal.","Two.","Closed.","Fluid."],"k":"Pleura: continuous double layer, closed space.","rn":[["","Pleural cavity","Pleural cavity","Closed space; Between visceral and parietal pleura; Contains pleural fluid"],["","How do the visceral and parietal layers relate?","How do the visceral and parietal layers relate?","Each continues into the other; One continuous layer"]]},{"id":17020,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"After a stab wound to the chest, blood collects between the visceral and parietal pleura. Name this.","e":"Blood = haemothorax; air = pneumothorax; pus = empyema; lymph (milky) = chylothorax; air + fluid = hydropneumothorax.","k":"Haemothorax.","a":["haemothorax","hemothorax"],"rn":[["","Haemothorax","Haemothorax","Blood in the pleural cavity"],["","Haemothorax vs chylothorax","Haemothorax vs chylothorax","Haemothorax: blood; Chylothorax: chyle"],["","Which 3 pleural collections involve blood, chyle or pus?","Which 3 pleural collections involve blood, chyle or pus?","Haemothorax; Chylothorax; Empyema (pyothorax)"]]},{"id":17021,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"A patient has both air and fluid in the pleural cavity. What is the term?","o":["Hydropneumothorax","Hydrothorax","Pneumothorax","Haemopneumothorax","Empyema"],"e":"<b>Air and fluid</b> = <b>hydropneumothorax</b>.","w":["Fluid only.","Air only.","Blood and air.","Pus."],"k":"Air + fluid = hydropneumothorax.","rn":[["","Hydropneumothorax","Hydropneumothorax","Air and fluid; In the pleural cavity"]]},{"id":17022,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"Pus in the pleural cavity is called what?","e":"Pus in the pleural cavity is an empyema.","k":"Empyema.","a":["empyema","pleural empyema","pyothorax"],"rn":[["","Empyema","Empyema","Pus in the pleural cavity"]]},{"id":17023,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","f":"bc26","s":"A chest radiograph shows a white opacity filling the lower right chest. Before further tests, what is the most appropriate general term?","o":["Pleural effusion – fluid between visceral and parietal pleura","Pleural effusion – fluid between parietal pleura and chest wall","Haemothorax – blood in the pleural cavity","Pneumothorax – air in the pleural cavity","Pericardial effusion – fluid around the heart"],"e":"<b>Pleural effusion</b> is a <b>general term</b> for an <b>abnormal accumulation of fluid</b> between the <b>visceral and parietal pleura</b>. At this stage it is simply an effusion (could be blood, chyle, pus, etc.) pending investigation. On a normal radiograph air is black; the diaphragm curves “like a parachute”; there is air in the stomach under it.","w":["Between pleural layers.","Not yet known.","Black, not white.","Not."],"k":"Pleural effusion = general term.","img":{"src":"17/effusion","cap":"Frontal chest radiograph."},"rn":[["","Pleural effusion","Pleural effusion","Abnormal fluid accumulation; Between visceral and parietal pleura"]]},{"id":17024,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","f":"bc27","s":"How does the pericardium differ from the pleura?","o":["An extra tough outer fibrous layer","An extra tough inner fibrous layer","No visceral layer","No fluid in its cavity","A single layer only"],"e":"The pericardium is a protective, fluid-filled sac around the <b>heart and great vessels</b>, exactly like the pleura (visceral, parietal, pericardial fluid, closed cavity) – with one exception: an extra tougher <b>fibrous pericardium</b>.","w":["Outer.","Present.","Fluid.","Layers."],"k":"Pericardium: extra fibrous layer.","rn":[["","What is the one exception that makes the pericardium different from the pleura?","What is the one exception that makes the pericardium different from the pleura?","An extra outer fibrous layer"]]},{"id":17025,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","f":"bc28","s":"In a dissection, why does the outer surface of the pericardial sac show the fibrous layer while its inner surface shows the parietal layer?","o":["The fibrous and parietal layers are adherent","The fibrous and visceral layers are adherent","The parietal layer covers the heart surface","The fibrous layer lines the inside of the sac","The layers are separated by CSF"],"e":"From inside you see the <b>parietal</b> layer; from outside the <b>fibrous</b> layer, because the two are <b>adherent</b>. The visceral layer is smooth on the heart surface. With the heart removed, the fibrous pericardium <b>blends with the great vessels</b>.","w":["Parietal.","Visceral.","Parietal outside.","Not."],"k":"Fibrous adherent to parietal.","rn":[["","Which 2 pericardial layers are adherent to each other?","Which 2 pericardial layers are adherent to each other?","Parietal layer; Fibrous pericardium"]]},{"id":17026,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","f":"bc30","s":"A patient is stabbed just below the right nipple at the level of the lower ribs. Why might abdominal organs be injured?","o":["The abdomen extends up to the diaphragm, which arches behind the lower ribs","The abdomen extends up to the diaphragm, which lies flat at the costal margin","The liver lies within the thoracic cavity","The ribs cover only the thoracic cavity","The abdomen lies above the diaphragm"],"e":"The abdominal cavity extends <b>superiorly to the diaphragm</b>, which <b>arches behind the lower ribs</b> like a parachute. A wound at the lower ribs may enter the <b>thorax, abdomen or both</b>, depending on whether the patient was breathing <b>in</b> (diaphragm moves down) or <b>out</b> (moves up).","w":["Arched.","Abdominal.","Both.","Below."],"k":"Diaphragm dome → lower rib wounds may enter abdomen.","rn":[["","Where does the diaphragm arch?","Where does the diaphragm arch?","Behind the lower ribs"]]},{"id":17027,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"On inspiration, what happens to the diaphragm and abdominal viscera?","o":["The diaphragm moves down, pushing viscera down","The diaphragm moves up, pulling viscera up","The diaphragm moves down, pulling viscera up","The viscera move into the thorax","Nothing changes"],"e":"On <b>inspiration</b> the diaphragm moves <b>down</b>, pushing the abdominal viscera down; on <b>expiration</b> it moves <b>up</b>.","w":["Down.","Pushed down.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Inspiration: diaphragm down.","rn":[["","What happens to the diaphragm during inspiration?","What happens to the diaphragm during inspiration?","It moves down; Pushes abdominal viscera down"]]},{"id":17028,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Which systems does the abdominal cavity house?","o":["Most of the gut, plus endocrine and urinary organs","Most of the digestive system, plus the heart and the lungs","The urinary system only","The spleen and liver only","The brain and spinal cord"],"e":"The abdominal cavity houses the <b>majority of the digestive system</b>, as well as organs of the <b>endocrine</b> and <b>urinary</b> systems.","w":["Thorax.","More.","More.","Cranial."],"k":"Abdomen contents.","rn":[["","What does the abdominal cavity house?","What does the abdominal cavity house?","Most of the digestive system; Endocrine organs; Urinary organs"]]},{"id":17029,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","f":"bc31","s":"Which planes divide the abdomen into four quadrants?","o":["A sagittal plane and the transumbilical plane","Two midclavicular and the subcostal plane","A sagittal plane and the subcostal plane","The transpyloric and intertubercular planes","A coronal plane and the transumbilical plane"],"e":"Four quadrants: a <b>sagittal</b> (vertical) plane and the <b>transumbilical</b> plane. Labels: liver (RUQ), spleen and stomach (LUQ), appendix (RLQ).","w":["Nine regions.","Transumbilical.","Not.","Sagittal."],"k":"Quadrants: sagittal + transumbilical.","rn":[["","Which 2 planes divide the abdomen into 4 quadrants?","Which 2 planes divide the abdomen into 4 quadrants?","Sagittal plane; Transumbilical plane"]]},{"id":17030,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","f":"bc32","s":"Which planes make the nine abdominal regions?","o":["Two midclavicular plus subcostal and intertubercular planes","Two midclavicular plus transumbilical and subcostal planes","One sagittal plus transumbilical plane","Three vertical planes only","Coronal planes only"],"e":"Nine regions: two <b>midclavicular</b> planes (vertical), the <b>subcostal</b> plane (just below the costal margin; the lecturer said L1) and the <b>intertubercular</b> plane. Nine regions give <b>more precise</b> localisation.","w":["Intertubercular.","Quadrants.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Nine regions planes.","rn":[["","Which 2 horizontal planes divide the abdomen?","Which 2 horizontal planes divide the abdomen?","Subcostal plane; Intertubercular plane"]]},{"id":17031,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Which region is labelled <b>B</b>, lateral to region A on the patient's right?","o":["Epigastric","Umbilical","Pubic","Right lumbar","Left iliac"],"e":"Top row: <b>right hypochondrium – epigastric – left hypochondrium</b>; middle: right lumbar – umbilical – left lumbar; bottom: right iliac – pubic (suprapubic/hypogastric) – left iliac.","w":["Middle row.","Bottom row.","Middle row lateral.","Bottom row lateral."],"k":"Epigastric = top middle.","img":{"src":"17/regions","cap":"Anterior abdominal wall divided by two midclavicular, subcostal and intertubercular planes. Labels removed."},"rn":[["","What are the 3 upper regions of the abdomen?","What are the 3 upper regions of the abdomen?","Right hypochondrium; Epigastric; Left hypochondrium"]]},{"id":17032,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"A patient points to midline pain just above the pubic symphysis. Name the region labelled <b>F</b>.","e":"F is the pubic (hypogastric) region, below the intertubercular plane in the midline; E and G are the right and left iliac regions.","k":"F = pubic (hypogastric).","img":{"src":"17/regions","cap":"Anterior abdominal wall divided by two midclavicular, subcostal and intertubercular planes. Labels removed."},"a":["pubic","hypogastric","suprapubic","hypogastrium","pubic region","hypogastric region"],"rn":[["","Pubic region","Pubic region","Suprapubic / hypogastric region"]]},{"id":17033,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"On a lower abdominal axial CT with pelvic bones but no ribs, two rounded structures beside the vertebra look like kidneys. Name these muscles.","e":"At this level they are the psoas muscles, not the kidneys.","k":"Psoas.","a":["psoas","psoas muscles","psoas major","iliopsoas"]},{"id":17034,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Name the part labelled <b>A</b>, which contains the gas bubble seen under the left dome of the diaphragm on a chest radiograph.","e":"A is the fundus. Other parts: cardia (B), body (C), pyloric antrum (F); lesser (D) and greater (E) curvatures.","k":"A = fundus.","img":{"src":"17/stomach","cap":"Stomach, anterior view. Labels removed."},"a":["fundus","fundus of stomach","gastric fundus"],"rn":[["","What are the 4 parts of the stomach?","What are the 4 parts of the stomach?","Fundus; Cardia; Body; Pyloric antrum"],["","Which part of the stomach lies under the left dome of the diaphragm?","Which part of the stomach lies under the left dome of the diaphragm?","Fundus"]]},{"id":17035,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","f":"bc35","s":"Which statement about the duodenum and pancreas is correct?","o":["C-shaped duodenum around the pancreatic head; pancreas behind stomach","C-shaped duodenum around the pancreatic tail; pancreas in front of stomach","J-shaped duodenum around the pancreatic head; pancreas behind stomach","C-shaped duodenum around the spleen; pancreas behind stomach","The pancreas is only exocrine"],"e":"<b>Duodenum</b>: <b>C-shaped first part</b> of the small intestine curving around the <b>head of the pancreas</b>. <b>Pancreas</b>: head, neck, body, tail; <b>both exocrine and endocrine</b>; lies mostly <b>behind the stomach</b>.","w":["Head; behind.","C-shaped.","Pancreas.","Both."],"k":"Duodenum C around pancreatic head.","rn":[["","Pancreas","Pancreas","Exocrine and endocrine gland; Head, neck, body, tail; Mostly behind the stomach"],["","What does the duodenum curve around?","What does the duodenum curve around?","The head of the pancreas"]]},{"id":17036,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Which pairing of kidney and its relation is correct?","o":["Right kidney partly behind duodenum; left behind pancreatic tail","Right kidney behind pancreatic tail; left partly behind duodenum","Right kidney behind spleen; left behind liver","Both kidneys in front of the pancreas","Left kidney behind duodenum; right behind stomach"],"e":"<b>Right kidney</b>: partly <b>behind the duodenum</b>. <b>Left kidney</b>: <b>behind the tail of the pancreas</b> (and stomach). “Remember this arrangement.”","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Behind.","Not."],"k":"Kidney relations.","rn":[["","Right vs left kidney: what lies in front","Right vs left kidney: what lies in front","Right: duodenum (partly); Left: tail of pancreas"]]},{"id":17037,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Where is the spleen and what type of organ is it?","o":["A lymphoid organ in the left upper quadrant under ribs 9–11","A lymphoid organ in the right upper quadrant under ribs 9–11","An endocrine organ in the left upper quadrant","A digestive gland behind the duodenum","A retroperitoneal organ near the kidney"],"e":"<b>Spleen</b>: <b>lymphoid organ</b> in the <b>left upper quadrant</b> beneath <b>ribs 9–11</b>. In the test-yourself coronal image, the spleen was number 3.","w":["Left.","Lymphoid.","Pancreas.","Intraperitoneal."],"k":"Spleen: LUQ, ribs 9–11.","rn":[["","Spleen","Spleen","Lymphoid organ; LUQ beneath ribs 9-11"]]},{"id":17038,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","f":"bc38","s":"Which statement describes a retroperitoneal organ?","o":["Behind the peritoneum on the back wall, covered only in front","Behind the peritoneum on the back wall, wrapped completely","Wrapped completely and hanging on a mesentery","Lying in the thoracic cavity","Floating freely in the cavity"],"e":"<b>Retroperitoneal</b>: behind the peritoneum, <b>directly on the posterior wall</b>, <b>anterior surface only</b> covered (kidneys, ureters, adrenals, pancreas, ascending and descending colon). <b>Intraperitoneal</b>: wrapped by visceral peritoneum and <b>suspended by a mesentery</b> (stomach, jejunum, ileum, transverse and sigmoid colon, spleen).","w":["Front only.","Intraperitoneal.","Abdomen.","Not."],"k":"Retro = behind, front covered.","rn":[["","Retroperitoneal","Retroperitoneal","Lies behind peritoneum; On posterior abdominal wall"],["","What does the visceral peritoneum do?","What does the visceral peritoneum do?","Covers the organs"]]},{"id":17039,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Which of these organs is intraperitoneal?","o":["Transverse colon","Ascending colon","Descending colon","Pancreas","Kidney"],"e":"Intraperitoneal: <b>stomach, jejunum, ileum, transverse colon, sigmoid colon, spleen</b>. Retroperitoneal: kidneys, ureters, adrenals, pancreas, ascending and descending colon.","w":["Retro.","Retro.","Retro.","Retro."],"k":"Transverse colon intraperitoneal.","rn":[["","Image card: Spotter 8: what is D?","Spotter 8: what is D?","Transverse colon"],["","Which 3 lower bowel parts are intraperitoneal?","Which 3 lower bowel parts are intraperitoneal?","Ileum; Transverse colon; Sigmoid colon"],["","Image card: Spotter 13: what is C?","Spotter 13: what is C?","Transverse colon"]]},{"id":17040,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Which of these organs is retroperitoneal?","o":["Descending colon","Sigmoid colon","Transverse colon","Jejunum","Spleen"],"e":"<b>Descending colon</b> is retroperitoneal; sigmoid colon, jejunum, spleen and stomach are intraperitoneal.","w":["Intra.","Intra.","Intra.","Intra."],"k":"Descending colon retroperitoneal.","rn":[["","Image card: Spotter 11: what is C?","Spotter 11: what is C?","Descending colon"],["","Which 3 gut organs are retroperitoneal?","Which 3 gut organs are retroperitoneal?","Pancreas; Ascending colon; Descending colon"],["","Ascending and descending colon are examples of what?","Ascending and descending colon are examples of what?","Retroperitoneal organs"]]},{"id":17041,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Name the double layer of peritoneum that suspends an intraperitoneal organ and carries its vessels, nerves and lymphatics.","e":"A mesentery is a double layer of peritoneum carrying vessels, nerves and lymphatics to intraperitoneal organs.","k":"Mesentery.","a":["mesentery","mesenteries"],"rn":[["","Mesentery","Mesentery","Double layer of peritoneum; Carries vessels, nerves and lymphatics"]]},{"id":17042,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Name the C-shaped organ labelled <b>B</b>, which curves around part A.","e":"B is the duodenum: the C-shaped first part of the small intestine, curving around the head of the pancreas (A).","k":"B = duodenum.","img":{"src":"17/pancreas","cap":"Upper abdominal organs, anterior view (liver and intestines removed). Labels removed."},"a":["duodenum"],"rn":[["","What is the duodenum?","What is the duodenum?","C-shaped; First part of small intestine"]]},{"id":17043,"t":"Embryonic Germ Layer Derivatives","s":"Which pairing of germ layer and derivative is correct?","o":["Ectoderm – epidermis, hair, nails","Ectoderm – dermis, hair, nails","Endoderm – bone and cartilage","Mesoderm – brain and spinal cord","Endoderm – heart and vessels"],"e":"<b>Ectoderm</b>: nervous system, epidermis/hair/nails/skin glands, lens/retina/inner ear, pituitary and adrenal medulla, enamel. <b>Mesoderm</b>: bone, cartilage, muscle, heart, vessels, blood, spleen, kidneys, ureters, gonads, dermis, serous membranes, adrenal cortex. <b>Endoderm</b>: gut lining, liver, gallbladder, pancreas, airway lining, thyroid, parathyroids, thymus, bladder/urethra lining.","w":["Dermis mesoderm.","Mesoderm.","Ectoderm.","Mesoderm."],"k":"Ectoderm: skin, nerves, senses.","rn":[["","Which parts of the integumentary system come from ectoderm?","Which parts of the integumentary system come from ectoderm?","Epidermis; Hair; Nails; Skin glands"]]},{"id":17044,"t":"Embryonic Germ Layer Derivatives","s":"Which germ layer gives rise to the adrenal medulla, and which to the adrenal cortex?","o":["Medulla – ectoderm; cortex – mesoderm","Medulla – mesoderm; cortex – ectoderm","Medulla – endoderm; cortex – mesoderm","Both – mesoderm","Both – ectoderm"],"e":"Watch the splits: <b>adrenal medulla = ectoderm</b>; <b>adrenal cortex = mesoderm</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Ectoderm.","Split.","Split."],"k":"Adrenal medulla ecto; cortex meso.","rn":[["","Adrenal medulla vs adrenal cortex: germ layer","Adrenal medulla vs adrenal cortex: germ layer","Medulla: ectoderm; Cortex: mesoderm"]]},{"id":17045,"t":"Embryonic Germ Layer Derivatives","s":"Which germ layer gives rise to the kidneys and ureters, and which to the lining of the bladder and urethra?","o":["Kidneys/ureters – mesoderm; bladder/urethra lining – endoderm","Kidneys/ureters – endoderm; bladder/urethra lining – mesoderm","Kidneys/ureters – mesoderm; bladder/urethra lining – mesoderm","Kidneys/ureters – ectoderm; bladder/urethra lining – endoderm","All urinary organs – endoderm"],"e":"<b>Kidneys and ureters = mesoderm</b>; <b>bladder and urethra lining = endoderm</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Endoderm.","Mesoderm.","Split."],"k":"Kidney meso; bladder lining endo.","rn":[["","Kidneys/ureters vs bladder/urethra lining: germ layer","Kidneys/ureters vs bladder/urethra lining: germ layer","Kidneys, ureters: mesoderm; Bladder, urethra lining: endoderm"],["","Which 2 germ layers give urinary structures?","Which 2 germ layers give urinary structures?","Mesoderm (kidneys, ureters); Endoderm (bladder, urethra lining)"]]},{"id":17046,"t":"Embryonic Germ Layer Derivatives","s":"Which germ layer gives rise to the serous membranes?","e":"Serous membranes derive from mesoderm.","k":"Mesoderm.","a":["mesoderm"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 embryonic germ layers?","What are the 3 embryonic germ layers?","Ectoderm; Mesoderm; Endoderm"]]},{"id":17047,"t":"Embryonic Germ Layer Derivatives","s":"Why is tracing organs back to germ layers clinically useful?","o":["It links malformations to one germ layer","It predicts which organs become infected","It determines which organs will be retroperitoneal","It predicts cancer stage","It determines blood group"],"e":"Tracing each organ or system back to its germ layer lets you link <b>congenital malformations</b> to a <b>single layer</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Germ layers → congenital malformations.","rn":[["","What are the 3 embryonic germ layers?","What are the 3 embryonic germ layers?","Ectoderm; Mesoderm; Endoderm"]]},{"id":17048,"t":"Embryonic Germ Layer Derivatives","s":"The thyroid, parathyroids and thymus arise from which germ layer?","o":["Endoderm","Ectoderm","Mesoderm","Neural crest","Trophoblast"],"e":"Endoderm endocrine derivatives: <b>thyroid, parathyroids, thymus</b>. Ectoderm endocrine: pituitary, adrenal medulla.","w":["Pituitary.","Adrenal cortex.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Thyroid/parathyroid/thymus = endoderm.","rn":[["","Which 2 germ layers give endocrine organs?","Which 2 germ layers give endocrine organs?","Ectoderm (pituitary, adrenal medulla); Endoderm (thyroid, parathyroids, thymus)"],["","Which endocrine organs come from endoderm?","Which endocrine organs come from endoderm?","Thyroid; Parathyroids; Thymus"]]},{"id":17049,"t":"Embryonic Germ Layer Derivatives","s":"Which tissue is ectodermal in origin?","o":["Tooth enamel","Dermis","Spleen","Gonads","Liver"],"e":"Ectoderm: <b>teeth: enamel</b>. Dermis, spleen, gonads = mesoderm; liver = endoderm.","w":["Mesoderm.","Mesoderm.","Mesoderm.","Endoderm."],"k":"Enamel = ectoderm.","rn":[["","Which part of the teeth comes from ectoderm?","Which part of the teeth comes from ectoderm?","Enamel"]]},{"id":17050,"t":"Embryonic Germ Layer Derivatives","s":"The epithelial lining of the larynx, trachea and lungs derives from which layer?","o":["Endoderm","Ectoderm","Mesoderm","Neural crest","Amnion"],"e":"Endoderm: <b>respiratory lining</b> of the larynx, trachea and lungs; also gut tube epithelium and accessory digestive organs.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Airway lining = endoderm.","rn":[["","Which respiratory part comes from endoderm?","Which respiratory part comes from endoderm?","Lining of larynx; Trachea and lungs"],["","Which digestive part comes from endoderm?","Which digestive part comes from endoderm?","Epithelial lining; Of the gut tube"]]},{"id":17051,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Name the region labelled <b>C</b>, containing the umbilicus between the subcostal and intertubercular planes.","e":"C is the umbilical region, flanked by the right (D) and left lumbar/flank regions.","k":"C = umbilical.","img":{"src":"17/regions","cap":"Anterior abdominal wall divided by two midclavicular, subcostal and intertubercular planes. Labels removed."},"a":["umbilical","umbilical region"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 middle regions of the abdomen?","What are the 3 middle regions of the abdomen?","Right lumbar (flank); Umbilical; Left lumbar (flank)"]]},{"id":17052,"t":"Cranial Cavity","s":"In a patient with meningitis, inflammation is in the space containing CSF. Between which layers is this?","o":["Between arachnoid and pia (subarachnoid space)","Between dura and arachnoid (potential space)","Between dura and skull (extradural space)","Between the two dural layers","Between pia and brain"],"e":"CSF lies in the <b>subarachnoid space</b>, between arachnoid and pia.","w":["No CSF.","No CSF.","Sinus.","Not."],"k":"CSF = subarachnoid.","rn":[["","Subarachnoid Space","Subarachnoid Space","Space between arachnoid and pia; Contains CSF and blood vessels"],["","What is the order of layers from bone to brain?","What is the order of layers from bone to brain?","Dura; Arachnoid; Subarachnoid space; Pia"]]},{"id":17053,"t":"Cranial Cavity","s":"A dissection of the back shows the spinal cord with an arrow labelled “pia mater” that seems to point at the cord itself. Why?","o":["The pia is too adherent to be seen separately","The pia is absent around the spinal cord","The label is simply wrong","The pia is the outermost layer there","The pia is fused to the dura"],"e":"The pia is hard to see because it is <b>adhered to the spinal cord</b>, so the arrow technically points at the cord.","w":["Present.","Adherent.","Innermost.","Not."],"k":"Pia adherent to cord."},{"id":17054,"t":"Body Cavities and Their Linings","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts the meninges with serous membranes?","o":["Meninges wrap brain and cord; serous membranes enclose a fluid-filled closed cavity","Meninges enclose a fluid-filled closed cavity; serous membranes wrap brain and cord","Both are found in the abdominal cavity","Both have three layers","Meninges contain serous fluid"],"e":"<b>Meninges</b> (dura, arachnoid, pia) line the cranial cavity and wrap the brain and spinal cord. <b>Serous membranes</b> (pleura, pericardium, peritoneum) have <b>visceral</b> and <b>parietal</b> layers enclosing a <b>closed cavity</b> with a thin film of fluid.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Two.","CSF."],"k":"Meninges vs serous membranes.","rn":[["","What are the 4 membranes that wrap organs in the body cavities?","What are the 4 membranes that wrap organs in the body cavities?","Meninges; Pleura; Pericardium; Peritoneum"],["","How do serous membranes reduce friction?","How do serous membranes reduce friction?","Fluid fills the space; Between the layers"],["","Meninges","Meninges","Brain and spinal cord"]]},{"id":17055,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> in the mediastinum?","o":["The lungs","The thoracic duct","The phrenic nerve","The aorta","The oesophagus"],"e":"The lungs are in the <b>pleural cavities</b>; the mediastinum is what lies between them.","w":["In mediastinum.","In mediastinum.","In mediastinum.","In mediastinum."],"k":"Lungs are not mediastinal.","rn":[["","On an axial section, what is the space between the lungs?","On an axial section, what is the space between the lungs?","The mediastinum"],["","Pleura","Pleura","Lung"],["","What is the serous cavity called around the lung?","What is the serous cavity called around the lung?","Pleural cavity (space)"]]},{"id":17056,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Which labelling of A, C and D is correct?","o":["A head of pancreas; C right kidney; D left kidney","A neck of pancreas; C right kidney; D left kidney","A head of pancreas; C left kidney; D right kidney","A head of pancreas; C right kidney; D spleen","A duodenum; C right kidney; D left kidney"],"e":"The head of the pancreas (A) sits in the curve of the duodenum. The right kidney (C) is partly behind the duodenum; the left kidney (D) lies behind the tail of the pancreas (E). The pancreas is exocrine and endocrine and lies mostly behind the stomach (F).","w":["The neck lies to the left of the head, over the vessels.","The diagram is anterior view: C is on the patient's right.","D lies behind the tail of the pancreas: the left kidney.","B is the duodenum; A sits within its curve."],"k":"Right kidney behind duodenum; left behind pancreatic tail.","img":{"src":"17/pancreas","cap":"Upper abdominal organs, anterior view (liver and intestines removed). Labels removed."},"rn":[["","Right vs left kidney: what lies in front","Right vs left kidney: what lies in front","Right: duodenum (partly); Left: tail of pancreas"]]},{"id":17057,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"A patient's abdominal CT shows the liver on the left side of the image. What does this confirm?","o":["Viewed from below; liver on the patient’s right","Viewed from above; liver on the patient’s right","Viewed from below; liver on the patient’s left","Situs inversus is present","It is a coronal image"],"e":"The side with the <b>liver</b> is the patient's <b>right</b> (on the left of the image), because axial images are viewed <b>from below</b>.","w":["Below.","Right.","Normal.","Axial."],"k":"Liver on image left = patient right.","rn":[["","Image card: Which side is left, and which side is right?","Which side is left, and which side is right?","Screen left: patient's right (liver); Screen right: patient's left; Viewed from below"],["","Image card: Which side of this axial CT is the patient's right? How can you tell?","Which side of this axial CT is the patient's right? How can you tell?","Screen left; Liver is on right; CT viewed from below"]]},{"id":17058,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Which quadrant contains the appendix labels?","o":["Right lower quadrant","Left lower quadrant","Right upper quadrant","Left upper quadrant","Epigastric region"],"e":"Labels: liver (right upper), spleen and stomach (left upper), <b>appendix (right lower)</b>.","w":["Right.","Liver.","Spleen.","Not."],"k":"Appendix RLQ.","rn":[["","Which quadrant is the appendix in?","Which quadrant is the appendix in?","Right lower quadrant"],["","RLQ","RLQ","Right lower quadrant"],["","What are the 4 quadrants of the abdomen?","What are the 4 quadrants of the abdomen?","Right upper; Left upper; Right lower; Left lower"]]},{"id":17059,"t":"Serous Membranes","s":"Which mechanism of serous membranes is described as reducing friction?","o":["Fluid between visceral and parietal layers","Air between visceral and parietal layers","Fibrous adhesions between the layers","CSF between the layers","Blood between the layers"],"e":"The space between visceral and parietal layers is filled with <b>fluid</b> (pleural, pericardial or peritoneal), <b>reducing friction</b>.","w":["Fluid.","Not.","CSF is cranial.","Not."],"k":"Serous fluid reduces friction.","rn":[["","How do serous membranes reduce friction?","How do serous membranes reduce friction?","Fluid fills the space; Between the layers"],["","What are the 2 layers of a serous membrane?","What are the 2 layers of a serous membrane?","Visceral layer; Parietal layer"],["","Pleural cavity","Pleural cavity","Closed space; Between visceral and parietal pleura; Contains pleural fluid"]]},{"id":17060,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"Which statement about “space” and “cavity” in serous membrane terminology is correct?","o":["They mean the same thing","“Space” is always larger","“Cavity” contains the organ; “space” does not","“Space” is cranial only","They are unrelated terms"],"e":"From the recording: “space” and “cavity” mean the same thing (pleural/pericardial/peritoneal cavity or space).","w":["Same.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Space = cavity.","rn":[["","What 2 other things can collect in the pleural cavity?","What 2 other things can collect in the pleural cavity?","Air; Chyle"]]},{"id":17061,"t":"Cranial Cavity","s":"A trauma patient has a head injury; CT shows blood between the skull and the outer dural layer. Which space is involved and why can blood collect there despite the dura being stuck to bone?","o":["Extradural – middle meningeal artery peels dura off bone","Subdural – venous bleeding separates dura from arachnoid","Subarachnoid – CSF pressure separates the layers","Extradural – a dural sinus peels dura off bone","Intraperitoneal – blood in the mesentery"],"e":"The <b>middle meningeal artery</b> lies between the periosteal dura and the bone; bleeding there <b>peels the dura away</b> from the skull, forming an <b>extradural haematoma</b>.","w":["Extradural.","Not.","Artery.","Not."],"k":"Extradural: MMA peels dura.","rn":[["","Extradural Haematoma","Extradural Haematoma","Bleeding outside the dura; From the middle meningeal artery; Peels dura off the skull"]]},{"id":17062,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Part <b>E</b> of an organ that is both exocrine and endocrine lies in front of the left kidney. Name the organ.","e":"E is the tail of the pancreas. The pancreas has a head, neck, body and tail; it is retroperitoneal and lies mostly behind the stomach.","k":"E = pancreas (tail).","img":{"src":"17/pancreas","cap":"Upper abdominal organs, anterior view (liver and intestines removed). Labels removed."},"a":["pancreas","tail of pancreas","tail of the pancreas","pancreatic tail"],"rn":[["","Pancreas","Pancreas","Exocrine and endocrine gland; Head, neck, body, tail; Mostly behind the stomach"],["","Right vs left kidney: what lies in front","Right vs left kidney: what lies in front","Right: duodenum (partly); Left: tail of pancreas"]]},{"id":17063,"t":"Serous Membranes","s":"Which pain pattern would inflammation of the parietal peritoneum typically produce?","o":["Sharp, well-localised pain (somatic supply)","Vague, referred pain (visceral supply)","No pain at all","Pain only in the head","Pain only on inspiration"],"e":"Parietal layers have <b>somatic sensory</b> innervation: pain usually <b>sharp and well localised</b>. Visceral layers: visceral sensory, poorly localised/referred.","w":["Visceral.","Pain.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Parietal = sharp localised.","rn":[["","Visceral vs parietal layer: pain","Visceral vs parietal layer: pain","Visceral: vague, referred; Parietal: sharp, localised"]]},{"id":17064,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Which of the following correctly lists retroperitoneal organs?","o":["Kidneys, ureters, adrenals, pancreas","Kidneys, ureters, adrenals, spleen","Stomach, spleen, jejunum","Ileum, transverse colon, sigmoid colon","Liver only"],"e":"Retroperitoneal: <b>kidneys, ureters, adrenal glands, pancreas, ascending and descending colon</b>.","w":["Spleen intra.","Intra.","Intra.","Not."],"k":"Retroperitoneal list.","rn":[["","Which 3 urinary/endocrine organs are retroperitoneal?","Which 3 urinary/endocrine organs are retroperitoneal?","Kidneys; Ureters; Adrenal glands"]]},{"id":17065,"t":"Serous Membranes","s":"Which statement best explains why visceral and parietal layers are “really one continuous layer”?","o":["Visceral reflects and continues as parietal","The visceral and parietal layers are glued together by CSF","They are the same cell layer as the dura","They are separated by bone","They have different embryological origins"],"e":"The visceral layer <b>reflects</b> and continues as the parietal layer (e.g. visceral pleura → parietal pleura), so it is really one continuous layer enclosing a closed space.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","One sac."],"k":"Reflection = continuity.","rn":[["","How do the visceral and parietal layers relate?","How do the visceral and parietal layers relate?","Each continues into the other; One continuous layer"]]},{"id":17066,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"After thoracic duct injury, milky fluid collects in the pleural cavity. Name the condition.","e":"Chylothorax: lymph (chyle) in the pleural cavity, from thoracic duct injury.","k":"Chylothorax.","a":["chylothorax"],"rn":[["","Chylothorax","Chylothorax","Chyle in the pleural cavity"]]},{"id":17067,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Which organ would you expect to be suspended by a mesentery?","o":["Ileum","Kidney","Pancreas","Ureter","Adrenal gland"],"e":"The <b>ileum</b> is intraperitoneal, suspended by a mesentery; the others are retroperitoneal.","w":["Retro.","Retro.","Retro.","Retro."],"k":"Ileum on mesentery.","rn":[["","Which 3 lower bowel parts are intraperitoneal?","Which 3 lower bowel parts are intraperitoneal?","Ileum; Transverse colon; Sigmoid colon"]]},{"id":17068,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"Which statement about the fibrous pericardium after removal of the heart is correct?","o":["It blends with the great vessels","It blends with the lungs, leaving an empty sac","It disappears with the heart","It becomes the diaphragm","It contains CSF"],"e":"With the heart removed, the empty pericardial sac shows the <b>fibrous pericardium blending with the great vessels</b>.","w":["Great vessels.","Remains.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Fibrous pericardium blends with great vessels.","rn":[["","Image card: The heart has been removed. What is this sac?","The heart has been removed. What is this sac?","Fibrous pericardium; Blends with the great vessels"]]},{"id":17069,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"A radiograph shows black areas in both lungs, a white heart, and a gas bubble under the left hemidiaphragm. What is the gas bubble?","o":["Air in the stomach under the left dome","Air in the pleural cavity (pneumothorax)","Air in the pericardium","Air in the right kidney","Air in the liver"],"e":"On the normal radiograph there is <b>air in the stomach</b> under the diaphragm; the fundus holds air under the <b>left dome</b>.","w":["Below diaphragm.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Gastric air under left dome.","rn":[["","Which part of the stomach lies under the left dome of the diaphragm?","Which part of the stomach lies under the left dome of the diaphragm?","Fundus"],["","What is the air under the left side of the diaphragm on a chest radiograph?","What is the air under the left side of the diaphragm on a chest radiograph?","Air in the stomach"]]},{"id":17070,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"Which statement about the diaphragm on a radiograph is correct?","o":["A curved partition between thorax and abdomen","A flat partition at the level of the pelvis","A vertical partition between the lungs","An invisible structure on radiographs","A partition between skull and neck"],"e":"The <b>diaphragm</b> is a curved partition between thorax and abdomen, “like a <b>parachute</b>”.","w":["Curved.","Horizontal-curved.","Visible.","Not."],"k":"Diaphragm: parachute-shaped.","rn":[["","What does the diaphragm separate?","What does the diaphragm separate?","Thorax; Abdomen"]]},{"id":17071,"t":"Cranial Cavity","s":"Which cavity does the arachnoid's web-like appearance relate to in naming?","o":["The subarachnoid space beneath the “spider” layer","The subdural space beneath the “hard” layer","The pleural cavity","The pericardial cavity","A dural sinus"],"e":"“Arachnoid” means <b>spider</b>; the space beneath it is the <b>sub</b>arachnoid space.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Arachnoid = spider.","rn":[["","What is the order of layers from bone to brain?","What is the order of layers from bone to brain?","Dura; Arachnoid; Subarachnoid space; Pia"],["","Subarachnoid Space","Subarachnoid Space","Space between arachnoid and pia; Contains CSF and blood vessels"]]},{"id":17072,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"Which statement about pleural fluid normally present is correct?","o":["A thin film of fluid that prevents friction","A large volume of fluid that cushions the lung","CSF that circulates around the lung","Blood that nourishes the pleura","No fluid at all"],"e":"Normally the pleural cavity contains a <b>thin film of fluid</b> to prevent friction. An abnormal accumulation = <b>pleural effusion</b>.","w":["Effusion.","Not.","Not.","Thin film."],"k":"Normal: thin film.","rn":[["","Is there fluid in a normal pleural cavity?","Is there fluid in a normal pleural cavity?","Yes, a thin film"],["","What is the job of normal pleural fluid?","What is the job of normal pleural fluid?","Prevents friction; Between the 2 layers"],["","What is the serous cavity?","What is the serous cavity?","Narrow potential space; Thin film of serous fluid"]]},{"id":17073,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"What makes the subcostal plane useful?","o":["Below the costal margin (L1); upper horizontal plane","At the umbilicus; the horizontal quadrant plane","Through the iliac crests; the lower horizontal plane of the nine","The vertical midclavicular plane","The plane dividing thorax from abdomen"],"e":"The <b>subcostal plane</b>: just below the costal margin; the lecturer said <b>L1</b>; upper horizontal plane of the nine regions (lower = intertubercular).","w":["Transumbilical.","Intertubercular.","Vertical.","Not."],"k":"Subcostal plane ≈ L1 (lecturer).","rn":[["","Where does the subcostal plane run?","Where does the subcostal plane run?","Just below the costal margin"]]},{"id":17074,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Which statement best explains why the abdominal organs appear to “rise up into the thorax”?","o":["The domed diaphragm arches behind the ribs","The liver is partly a thoracic organ above the diaphragm","The ribs cover only the abdomen","The diaphragm lies below the pelvis","The heart sits in the abdomen"],"e":"The diaphragm is <b>parachute-shaped</b>; abdominal organs under it rise up, and the ribs overlie both cavities; the thoracic cavity forms a <b>ring</b> around the top of the abdominal cavity at this level.","w":["Abdominal.","Both.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Diaphragm dome under ribs.","rn":[["","Where does the diaphragm arch?","Where does the diaphragm arch?","Behind the lower ribs"]]},{"id":17075,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"A surgeon wants to reach the organ whose tail is labelled E from the front. Which organ lies mostly anterior to it?","o":["The stomach","The left kidney","The spleen","The aorta","The right kidney"],"e":"The pancreas lies <b>mostly behind the stomach</b>.","w":["Behind pancreatic tail.","Lateral LUQ.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Stomach anterior to pancreas.","img":{"src":"17/pancreas","cap":"Upper abdominal organs, anterior view (liver and intestines removed). Labels removed."},"rn":[["","Pancreas","Pancreas","Exocrine and endocrine gland; Head, neck, body, tail; Mostly behind the stomach"],["","Image card: Spotter 8: what is C?","Spotter 8: what is C?","Stomach"]]},{"id":17076,"t":"Serous Membranes","s":"Which explanation of “referred pain” connects to visceral layers?","o":["Visceral sensory supply gives poorly localised, referred pain","Somatic sensory supply gives poorly localised, referred pain","Parietal layers produce referred pain","Only the dura produces referred pain","Referred pain does not occur"],"e":"Visceral layers: <b>visceral sensory</b>, pain usually <b>poorly localised</b> or <b>referred</b>; this becomes important later.","w":["Somatic is sharp.","Somatic.","Not.","It does."],"k":"Visceral → referred pain.","rn":[["","Visceral vs parietal layer: pain","Visceral vs parietal layer: pain","Visceral: vague, referred; Parietal: sharp, localised"]]},{"id":17077,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Which statement about this organ is correct?","o":["J-shaped sac in the left upper quadrant with two curvatures","J-shaped sac in the right upper quadrant with two curvatures","C-shaped sac around the pancreatic head","A retroperitoneal muscular sac","A lymphoid organ in the left upper quadrant"],"e":"Stomach: <b>J-shaped muscular sac</b> in the <b>left upper quadrant</b>; fundus, cardia, body, pyloric antrum; lesser and greater curvatures; intraperitoneal.","w":["Left.","Duodenum.","Intra.","Spleen."],"k":"Stomach: J-shaped, LUQ.","img":{"src":"17/stomach","cap":"Stomach, anterior view. Labels removed."},"rn":[["","Stomach","Stomach","J-shaped muscular sac; Left upper quadrant"]]},{"id":17078,"t":"Embryonic Germ Layer Derivatives","s":"Which pair is derived from mesoderm?","o":["Heart and gonads","Brain and retina","Liver and pancreas","Epidermis and nails","Thyroid and thymus"],"e":"Mesoderm: musculoskeletal, <b>heart</b>, vessels, blood, spleen, kidneys, ureters, <b>gonads</b>, genital ducts, dermis, serous membranes, adrenal cortex.","w":["Ectoderm.","Endoderm.","Ectoderm.","Endoderm."],"k":"Heart, gonads = mesoderm.","rn":[["","Which reproductive structures come from mesoderm?","Which reproductive structures come from mesoderm?","Gonads; Genital ducts"]]},{"id":17079,"t":"Embryonic Germ Layer Derivatives","s":"Which pair is derived from endoderm?","o":["Liver and gallbladder","Spleen and blood","Kidneys and ureters","Lens and inner ear","Dermis and bone"],"e":"Endoderm: gut tube epithelium, <b>liver, gallbladder</b>, pancreas, airway lining, thyroid, parathyroids, thymus, bladder/urethra lining.","w":["Mesoderm.","Mesoderm.","Ectoderm.","Mesoderm."],"k":"Liver, gallbladder = endoderm.","rn":[["","Which accessory digestive organs come from endoderm?","Which accessory digestive organs come from endoderm?","Liver; Gallbladder; Pancreas"]]},{"id":17080,"t":"Embryonic Germ Layer Derivatives","s":"Which split is correct about skin layers and germ layers?","o":["Epidermis – ectoderm; dermis – mesoderm","Epidermis – mesoderm; dermis – ectoderm","Epidermis and dermis – ectoderm","Epidermis and dermis – mesoderm","Epidermis – endoderm; dermis – mesoderm"],"e":"<b>Dermis = mesoderm</b> but <b>epidermis = ectoderm</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Dermis mesoderm.","Epidermis ectoderm.","Ectoderm."],"k":"Epidermis ecto; dermis meso.","rn":[["","Epidermis vs dermis: germ layer","Epidermis vs dermis: germ layer","Epidermis: ectoderm; Dermis: mesoderm"]]},{"id":17081,"t":"Serous Membranes","s":"The pericardial cavity lies between which layers?","o":["Visceral and parietal serous layers","Fibrous and parietal layers","Visceral layer and the heart","Fibrous pericardium and the pleura","Dura and arachnoid"],"e":"The <b>pericardial cavity</b> lies between the <b>visceral</b> and <b>parietal</b> serous layers, containing <b>pericardial fluid</b>; the fibrous layer is adherent to the parietal.","w":["Adherent.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Pericardial cavity: visceral–parietal.","rn":[["","What are the 2 layers of a serous membrane?","What are the 2 layers of a serous membrane?","Visceral layer; Parietal layer"],["","Which 2 pericardial layers are adherent to each other?","Which 2 pericardial layers are adherent to each other?","Parietal layer; Fibrous pericardium"]]},{"id":17082,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"Which combination of nerves passes through the mediastinum?","o":["Vagus and phrenic nerves","Optic and olfactory nerves","Sciatic and femoral nerves","Facial and trigeminal nerves","Median and ulnar nerves"],"e":"Mediastinal nerves: <b>vagus</b> and <b>phrenic</b>; lymphatic: thoracic duct.","w":["Cranial.","Lower limb.","Cranial.","Upper limb."],"k":"Vagus, phrenic in mediastinum.","rn":[["","What are the 3 nerves and lymphatics of the mediastinum?","What are the 3 nerves and lymphatics of the mediastinum?","Vagus nerve; Phrenic nerve; Thoracic duct"]]},{"id":17083,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Name the border labelled <b>D</b>, the short concave right border of the stomach.","e":"D is the lesser curvature; E is the greater curvature.","k":"D = lesser curvature.","img":{"src":"17/stomach","cap":"Stomach, anterior view. Labels removed."},"a":["lesser curvature","lesser curve"],"rn":[["","What are the 2 curvatures of the stomach?","What are the 2 curvatures of the stomach?","Lesser curvature; Greater curvature"]]},{"id":17086,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Which organ in the right upper quadrant is the key landmark for orientation on abdominal axial images?","o":["The liver","The spleen","The sigmoid colon","The appendix","The bladder"],"e":"The <b>liver</b> marks the patient's <b>right</b>; use landmarks such as liver, ribs and bones.","w":["Left.","Left lower.","Right lower.","Pelvis."],"k":"Liver = right landmark.","rn":[["","Which quadrant is the liver in?","Which quadrant is the liver in?","Right upper quadrant"],["","Which landmarks help you find the level of an axial section?","Which landmarks help you find the level of an axial section?","Liver; Ribs; Bones (e.g. pelvis, vertebra)"],["","Image card: Spotter 8: what is G?","Spotter 8: what is G?","Liver"]]},{"id":17087,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"A patient has fluid collecting in the peritoneal cavity. Between which layers is the fluid?","o":["Visceral and parietal peritoneum","Visceral and parietal pleura","Two layers of a mesentery","Fibrous and parietal pericardium","Dura and arachnoid"],"e":"The peritoneal cavity is between <b>visceral</b> (covering organs) and <b>parietal</b> (lining the abdominal cavity) peritoneum.","w":["Thorax.","Mesentery carries vessels.","Heart.","Brain."],"k":"Peritoneal cavity layers.","rn":[["","What is the space between visceral and parietal peritoneum?","What is the space between visceral and parietal peritoneum?","Peritoneal cavity"]]},{"id":17088,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"What runs between the two layers of a mesentery?","o":["Vessels, nerves, lymphatics","Vessels, nerves and CSF","Air and serous fluid","Pus, normally","Bone"],"e":"Vessels, nerves and lymphatics run between the two layers of a mesentery.","w":["Lymphatics.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Mesentery contents.","rn":[["","What are the 3 groups of mediastinal contents?","What are the 3 groups of mediastinal contents?","Viscera; Vessels; Nerves and lymphatics"],["","Mesentery","Mesentery","Double layer of peritoneum; Carries vessels, nerves and lymphatics"]]},{"id":17089,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Which statement about what an axial section at the level of the upper abdomen typically shows is correct?","o":["Liver on the patient’s right, kidneys posteriorly, gas in bowel","Liver on the patient’s left, kidneys anteriorly, gas in bowel","Heart and lungs only","Pelvic bones and psoas only","Bladder and uterus"],"e":"Upper abdominal section: <b>liver</b> on patient's right; <b>kidneys</b> at the back near the vertebra; black = air in stomach/bowel.","w":["Right; posterior.","Thorax.","Lower.","Pelvis."],"k":"Upper abdominal axial features.","rn":[["","Which organs can be seen on an upper abdominal axial CT?","Which organs can be seen on an upper abdominal axial CT?","Liver; Kidneys; Vertebra; Stomach (air)"]]},{"id":17090,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Which statement about the pancreas's dual function is correct?","o":["It is both exocrine and endocrine","It is only endocrine","It is only exocrine","It is a lymphoid organ","It is a smooth muscle organ"],"e":"Pancreas: <b>both an exocrine and an endocrine gland</b>.","w":["Both.","Both.","Spleen.","Not."],"k":"Pancreas exocrine + endocrine.","rn":[["","Pancreas","Pancreas","Exocrine and endocrine gland; Head, neck, body, tail; Mostly behind the stomach"]]},{"id":17091,"t":"Cranial Cavity","s":"A boxer's blow to the temple causes a lucid interval then deterioration; CT shows a lens-shaped collection. Which layer has been separated from the bone?","o":["The outer periosteal layer of the dura","The pia mater","The arachnoid","The inner meningeal layer from the arachnoid","The visceral pleura"],"e":"An extradural haematoma from the middle meningeal artery peels the <b>periosteal (outer) dura</b> from the skull (bone on one side, dura on the other).","w":["Adheres to brain.","Below dura.","Not.","Thorax."],"k":"Extradural: periosteal dura separated.","rn":[["","Periosteal vs meningeal layer of dura: position","Periosteal vs meningeal layer of dura: position","Periosteal: outer, on bone; Meningeal: inner"],["","What are the 2 layers of the dura mater in the cranial cavity?","What are the 2 layers of the dura mater in the cranial cavity?","Outer periosteal (endosteal) layer; Inner meningeal layer"],["","Where does the middle meningeal artery run?","Where does the middle meningeal artery run?","Between periosteal (outer) dura; And cranial bones"]]},{"id":17092,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Name the part labelled <b>F</b>, leading to the pylorus and duodenum.","e":"F is the pyloric antrum, which leads to the pylorus.","k":"F = pyloric antrum.","img":{"src":"17/stomach","cap":"Stomach, anterior view. Labels removed."},"a":["pyloric antrum","antrum","pylorus","pyloric part"],"rn":[["","What are the 4 parts of the stomach?","What are the 4 parts of the stomach?","Fundus; Cardia; Body; Pyloric antrum"]]},{"id":17093,"t":"Cranial Cavity","s":"Which statement about how the dura relates to the spinal cord is correct?","o":["Meninges continue around the cord, dura outermost","The meninges stop at the foramen magnum","Only the pia surrounds the spinal cord","The dura lies inside the pia around the spinal cord","The cord is wrapped by pleura"],"e":"The meninges <b>continue down around the spinal cord</b>: pia on the cord, arachnoid, dura as the outer tube.","w":["Continue.","All three.","Outermost.","Not."],"k":"Meninges around cord.","rn":[["","What are the 3 meningeal layers around the spinal cord?","What are the 3 meningeal layers around the spinal cord?","Dura mater; Arachnoid mater; Pia mater"],["","Where do the meninges start and go?","Where do the meninges start and go?","Start at the brain; Down around spinal cord"],["","Meninges","Meninges","Brain and spinal cord"]]},{"id":17094,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Which pair of quadrant locations is correct for the liver and spleen?","o":["Liver – right upper; spleen – left upper","Liver – left upper; spleen – right upper","Liver – right lower; spleen – left lower","Liver – right upper; spleen – left lower","Both in the right upper quadrant"],"e":"Liver: right upper quadrant; spleen and stomach: left upper quadrant.","w":["Swapped.","Upper.","Upper.","Different."],"k":"Liver RUQ; spleen LUQ.","rn":[["","Which quadrant is the spleen in?","Which quadrant is the spleen in?","Left upper quadrant"],["","Which quadrant is the liver in?","Which quadrant is the liver in?","Right upper quadrant"],["","RUQ","RUQ","Right upper quadrant"]]},{"id":17095,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Which relation between abdominal organs is correct?","o":["Remember relations, e.g. left kidney behind pancreas","Remember only organ names, not their relations","Remember only the four quadrants","Organs move randomly, so their relations do not matter much","Remember only retroperitoneal organs"],"e":"“Remember this <b>arrangement</b>”: anatomical relations matter, e.g. left kidney behind stomach/pancreatic tail.","w":["Relations.","Not only.","Relations matter.","Not only."],"k":"Learn relations.","rn":[["","Right vs left kidney: what lies in front","Right vs left kidney: what lies in front","Right: duodenum (partly); Left: tail of pancreas"]]},{"id":17096,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"The heart is wrapped by which pericardial layers from inside outward?","o":["Visceral → cavity → parietal → fibrous","Fibrous → parietal → cavity → visceral","Visceral → cavity → fibrous → parietal","Parietal → cavity → visceral → fibrous","Visceral → parietal → cavity → fibrous"],"e":"Inside out: <b>visceral</b> (on heart), pericardial cavity with fluid, <b>parietal</b>, then <b>fibrous</b> (adherent to parietal).","w":["Outside in.","Fibrous outermost.","Visceral on heart.","Cavity between."],"k":"Pericardial layers order.","rn":[["","Which 2 pericardial layers are adherent to each other?","Which 2 pericardial layers are adherent to each other?","Parietal layer; Fibrous pericardium"],["","What are the 3 layers of the pericardium, outside in?","What are the 3 layers of the pericardium, outside in?","Fibrous pericardium; Parietal layer; Visceral layer"],["","What is the space between visceral and parietal peritoneum?","What is the space between visceral and parietal peritoneum?","Peritoneal cavity"]]},{"id":17097,"t":"Embryonic Germ Layer Derivatives","s":"Which of the following is an ectodermal special-sense derivative?","o":["Retina","Spleen","Thymus","Kidney","Bladder lining"],"e":"Ectoderm special senses: <b>lens, retina, inner ear</b>.","w":["Mesoderm.","Endoderm.","Mesoderm.","Endoderm."],"k":"Retina = ectoderm.","rn":[["","Which special senses structures come from ectoderm?","Which special senses structures come from ectoderm?","Lens; Retina; Inner ear"]]},{"id":17098,"t":"Embryonic Germ Layer Derivatives","s":"Which statement about the urinary tract lining and germ layers is a common exam trap?","o":["Kidneys/ureters mesoderm, but bladder/urethra lining endoderm","Kidneys/ureters endoderm, but bladder/urethra lining mesoderm","All urinary organs ectoderm","Bladder and kidneys both mesoderm","Urethra ectoderm, kidneys endoderm"],"e":"Watch the split: kidneys/ureters = mesoderm; bladder/urethra lining = endoderm.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Lining endoderm.","Not."],"k":"Urinary split.","rn":[["","Kidneys/ureters vs bladder/urethra lining: germ layer","Kidneys/ureters vs bladder/urethra lining: germ layer","Kidneys, ureters: mesoderm; Bladder, urethra lining: endoderm"],["","Which 2 germ layers give urinary structures?","Which 2 germ layers give urinary structures?","Mesoderm (kidneys, ureters); Endoderm (bladder, urethra lining)"]]},{"id":17099,"t":"Embryonic Germ Layer Derivatives","s":"Which germ layer gives rise to the thyroid, parathyroids and thymus?","o":["Endoderm","Mesoderm","Ectoderm","Neural crest","Endoderm for thyroid; mesoderm for thymus"],"e":"Endoderm forms the gut tube epithelium, liver, gallbladder, pancreas, airway lining, thyroid, parathyroids, thymus and the lining of the bladder and urethra.","w":["Mesoderm forms bone, muscle, heart, vessels, kidneys.","Ectoderm forms nervous system, epidermis, lens, enamel.","Not one of the three listed layers in this lecture.","All three are endodermal."],"k":"Session order.","rn":[["","Which 2 germ layers give endocrine organs?","Which 2 germ layers give endocrine organs?","Ectoderm (pituitary, adrenal medulla); Endoderm (thyroid, parathyroids, thymus)"],["","Which endocrine organs come from endoderm?","Which endocrine organs come from endoderm?","Thyroid; Parathyroids; Thymus"],["","What are the 3 embryonic germ layers?","What are the 3 embryonic germ layers?","Ectoderm; Mesoderm; Endoderm"]]},{"id":17100,"t":"Cranial Cavity","s":"Which statement about the dura–arachnoid interface is correct?","o":["A potential space that appears only with bleeding","A space normally containing CSF","The extradural space containing the middle meningeal artery","A serous cavity with a thin fluid film","A dural venous sinus"],"e":"The arachnoid is attached to the dura, so the space between is only <b>potential</b>, appearing with bleeding.","w":["Subarachnoid.","Extradural.","Not serous.","Sinus."],"k":"Dura–arachnoid: potential space.","rn":[["","What is the space between the dura and arachnoid?","What is the space between the dura and arachnoid?","Only a potential space"]]},{"id":17101,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"Which structure seen on the upper thoracic axial section marks the posterior side?","o":["The vertebra","The sternum","The heart","The liver","The stomach"],"e":"The <b>vertebra</b> tells you the back (posterior); sternum is anterior.","w":["Anterior.","Not.","Abdomen.","Abdomen."],"k":"Vertebra = posterior.","rn":[["","Which bone tells you anterior from posterior on an axial section?","Which bone tells you anterior from posterior on an axial section?","The vertebra (posterior)"]]},{"id":17102,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"A peritoneal infection spreads freely around organs that hang on mesenteries. Which organ group would most readily be bathed in this peritoneal fluid?","o":["Intraperitoneal organs such as stomach, jejunum and ileum","Retroperitoneal organs such as kidneys and pancreas","Thoracic organs such as the lungs","The brain and spinal cord","The adrenal glands"],"e":"Intraperitoneal organs are wrapped and hang into the peritoneal cavity; retroperitoneal organs sit on the posterior wall with only the front covered.","w":["Behind.","Thorax.","Not.","Retro."],"k":"Intraperitoneal organs hang into cavity.","rn":[["","Which 3 upper organs are intraperitoneal?","Which 3 upper organs are intraperitoneal?","Stomach; Jejunum; Spleen"]]},{"id":17103,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Which statement about the spleen's peritoneal status is correct?","o":["It is intraperitoneal","It is retroperitoneal","It is thoracic","It has no peritoneum","It is in the mesentery only"],"e":"Spleen: <b>intraperitoneal</b> (listed with stomach, jejunum, ileum, transverse and sigmoid colon).","w":["Intra.","Abdominal.","Covered.","Not."],"k":"Spleen intraperitoneal.","rn":[["","Which 3 upper organs are intraperitoneal?","Which 3 upper organs are intraperitoneal?","Stomach; Jejunum; Spleen"],["","Which 3 lower bowel parts are intraperitoneal?","Which 3 lower bowel parts are intraperitoneal?","Ileum; Transverse colon; Sigmoid colon"]]},{"id":17104,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Appendicitis pain settles in the region labelled <b>E</b>. Name it.","e":"E is the right iliac region (in four-quadrant terms, the right lower quadrant, where the appendix lies).","k":"E = right iliac.","img":{"src":"17/regions","cap":"Anterior abdominal wall divided by two midclavicular, subcostal and intertubercular planes. Labels removed."},"a":["right iliac","right iliac fossa","right iliac region","rif","right inguinal"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 lower regions of the abdomen?","What are the 3 lower regions of the abdomen?","Right iliac; Pubic; Left iliac"]]},{"id":17105,"t":"Cranial Cavity","s":"Which statement about the cranial cavity is correct?","o":["The part of the skull containing the brain, wrapped by meninges","The part of the skull containing the brain, lined by serous membrane","A cavity containing the heart","A cavity lined by peritoneum","Part of the mediastinum"],"e":"Cranial cavity (cranium): the part of the skull containing the brain; meninges wrap the brain and spinal cord.","w":["Meninges.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Cranial cavity.","rn":[["","Cranial cavity","Cranial cavity","Skull part holding the brain; Lined by meninges"]]},{"id":17106,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"For which physiological process is the closed pleural space especially important?","o":["Breathing, with the pleura","Digestion, with the peritoneum","Heart filling, with the pericardium","Hearing, with the meninges","Vision, with the meninges"],"e":"The closed pleural space matters for <b>breathing</b>, outside the scope of this lecture.","w":["Pleura.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Closed pleural space → breathing.","rn":[["","Pleural cavity","Pleural cavity","Closed space; Between visceral and parietal pleura; Contains pleural fluid"]]},{"id":17107,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Which statement about the lower abdominal axial section is correct?","o":["Pelvic bones and psoas, but no ribs","Ribs and liver, but no pelvic bones","The heart and great vessels","The spleen beneath ribs 9–11","The gastric fundus"],"e":"Lower section: <b>pelvic bones</b> and <b>psoas</b>; no ribs.","w":["Upper.","Thorax.","Upper.","Upper."],"k":"Lower section features.","rn":[["","Upper thoracic axial section: which 3 bones?","Upper thoracic axial section: which 3 bones?","Sternum; Ribs (parts); Vertebra"],["","Which landmarks help you find the level of an axial section?","Which landmarks help you find the level of an axial section?","Liver; Ribs; Bones (e.g. pelvis, vertebra)"]]},{"id":17108,"t":"Serous Membranes","s":"What is the general principle of naming serous cavities?","o":["After the organ","After the bone nearby","After the colour of their fluid","After their nerve supply","After their discoverer"],"e":"Serous cavities are <b>named after the organ</b>: pleural, pericardial, peritoneal.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Named after organ.","rn":[["","What is the serous cavity called around abdominal organs?","What is the serous cavity called around abdominal organs?","Peritoneal cavity (space)"]]},{"id":17109,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"Which statement about the pericardium's function is correct?","o":["A protective fluid-filled sac around the heart","A protective, fluid-filled sac around the lungs and bronchi","A layer that produces CSF","A sheet that suspends the intestines","A lining of the abdominal wall"],"e":"Pericardium: <b>protective, fluid-filled sac</b> surrounding the heart and great vessels.","w":["Pleura.","Not.","Mesentery.","Peritoneum."],"k":"Pericardium protects heart.","rn":[["","What is the pericardium?","What is the pericardium?","Protective fluid-filled sac; Around heart, great vessels"]]},{"id":17110,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Which statement about the right lumbar region is correct?","o":["Middle row, lateral to the umbilical region, on the right","Top row, lateral to the epigastric region, on the right","Bottom row, lateral to the pubic region, on the right","Top middle region","Bottom middle region"],"e":"Middle row: <b>right lumbar/flank – umbilical – left lumbar/flank</b>.","w":["Hypochondrium.","Iliac.","Epigastric.","Pubic."],"k":"Lumbar = middle row lateral.","rn":[["","What are the 3 middle regions of the abdomen?","What are the 3 middle regions of the abdomen?","Right lumbar (flank); Umbilical; Left lumbar (flank)"]]},{"id":17111,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"A gunshot passes through the lower chest during expiration. Which statement best predicts injury?","o":["Abdominal organs, as the diaphragm is raised in expiration","Abdominal organs, as the diaphragm is lowered in expiration","Only the lungs, as the diaphragm protects the abdomen","Only the brain","No organs"],"e":"On <b>expiration</b> the diaphragm moves <b>up</b>, so abdominal organs sit higher under the lower ribs; on inspiration they are pushed down.","w":["Raised.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Expiration: viscera higher.","rn":[["","What happens to the diaphragm during expiration?","What happens to the diaphragm during expiration?","It moves up"],["","What happens to the diaphragm during inspiration?","What happens to the diaphragm during inspiration?","It moves down; Pushes abdominal viscera down"]]},{"id":17112,"t":"Embryonic Germ Layer Derivatives","s":"Which cells/structures are listed as ectoderm endocrine derivatives?","o":["Pituitary and adrenal medulla","Thyroid and thymus","Adrenal cortex and gonads","Pancreatic islets","Parathyroids and thymus"],"e":"Ectoderm endocrine: <b>pituitary gland, adrenal medulla</b>.","w":["Endoderm.","Mesoderm.","Endoderm.","Endoderm."],"k":"Pituitary, adrenal medulla = ectoderm.","rn":[["","Which endocrine organs come from ectoderm?","Which endocrine organs come from ectoderm?","Pituitary gland; Adrenal medulla"],["","Which 2 germ layers give endocrine organs?","Which 2 germ layers give endocrine organs?","Ectoderm (pituitary, adrenal medulla); Endoderm (thyroid, parathyroids, thymus)"]]},{"id":17113,"t":"Embryonic Germ Layer Derivatives","s":"Which of the following is mesodermal: spleen, liver, retina, thyroid, or epidermis?","o":["Spleen","Liver","Retina","Thyroid","Epidermis"],"e":"Mesoderm: cardiovascular and lymphatic – heart, vessels, blood, <b>spleen</b>.","w":["Endoderm.","Ectoderm.","Endoderm.","Ectoderm."],"k":"Spleen = mesoderm.","rn":[["","Spleen vs liver: germ layer","Spleen vs liver: germ layer","Spleen: mesoderm; Liver: endoderm"],["","Which cardiovascular and lymphatic structures come from mesoderm?","Which cardiovascular and lymphatic structures come from mesoderm?","Heart; Vessels; Blood; Spleen"]]},{"id":17114,"t":"Cranial Cavity","s":"Which statement about the location of the middle meningeal artery is correct?","o":["At the side of the head, between periosteal dura and bone","At the side of the head, between the two dural layers","In the subarachnoid space","On the surface of the pia","In the mediastinum"],"e":"<b>Middle meningeal artery</b>: between periosteal (outer) dura and cranial bones, at the <b>side of the head</b>.","w":["Outside dura.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"MMA: side of head, outside dura.","rn":[["","Where does the middle meningeal artery run?","Where does the middle meningeal artery run?","Between periosteal (outer) dura; And cranial bones"],["","Periosteal vs meningeal layer of dura: position","Periosteal vs meningeal layer of dura: position","Periosteal: outer, on bone; Meningeal: inner"]]},{"id":17115,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"What is a “potential space”?","o":["Empty until fluid or blood collects","A large air-filled space","A space filled with bone","A space that is present only in embryos","A space within cells"],"e":"Serous cavities are narrow <b>potential</b> spaces with a thin film of fluid; the dura–arachnoid space is potential, appearing with bleeding.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Potential space concept.","rn":[["","Which 3 fluids can collect in the pleural cavity?","Which 3 fluids can collect in the pleural cavity?","Fluid; Blood; Pus"]]},{"id":17116,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"Only air is present in the pleural cavity. Name the condition.","e":"Pneumothorax: air in the pleural cavity.","k":"Pneumothorax.","a":["pneumothorax"],"rn":[["","Pneumothorax","Pneumothorax","Air in the pleural cavity"]]},{"id":17117,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"Which organ curves in a C-shape and is the first part of the small intestine?","o":["Duodenum","Jejunum","Ileum","Transverse colon","Stomach"],"e":"<b>Duodenum</b>: C-shaped first part of the small intestine around the pancreatic head.","w":["Later.","Last.","Large bowel.","J-shaped."],"k":"Duodenum C-shaped.","rn":[["","What is the duodenum?","What is the duodenum?","C-shaped; First part of small intestine"]]},{"id":17118,"t":"Body Cavities and Their Linings","s":"Which statement about body cavities and their linings is correct?","o":["Membranes and sheaths separate body compartments","Bones alone organise the body into compartments","Organs float freely in cavities without separating membranes","Serous membranes line the brain","Meninges line every cavity"],"e":"Key message: internal organisation via <b>membranes, sheaths and structures separating compartments</b>; cranial cavity – meninges; thoracic and abdominal – serous membranes.","w":["Membranes.","Not.","Meninges.","Not."],"k":"Membranes organise compartments.","rn":[["","How does the body maintain its internal organisation?","How does the body maintain its internal organisation?","Membranes; Sheaths; Other structures that separate compartments"]]},{"id":17119,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"Which statement is correct about the pericardial layers seen in an opened sac?","o":["Visceral on heart, parietal inside sac, fibrous outside","Fibrous on heart, parietal inside sac, visceral outside","Parietal on heart, visceral inside sac, fibrous outside","Visceral on heart, fibrous inside sac, parietal outside","There is no visceral layer"],"e":"Opened sac: <b>1</b> visceral on heart; <b>2</b> fibrous layer; <b>3</b> parietal layer.","w":["Swapped.","Swapped.","Swapped.","There is."],"k":"Opened pericardium layers.","rn":[["","What are the 3 layers of the pericardium, outside in?","What are the 3 layers of the pericardium, outside in?","Fibrous pericardium; Parietal layer; Visceral layer"],["","Seen from outside, what layer forms the pericardial sac?","Seen from outside, what layer forms the pericardial sac?","Fibrous pericardium"]]},{"id":17120,"t":"Embryonic Germ Layer Derivatives","s":"Which germ layer gives rise to the lens, retina and inner ear?","o":["Ectoderm","Mesoderm","Endoderm","Ectoderm for lens; mesoderm for retina","Endoderm for inner ear; ectoderm for lens"],"e":"Ectoderm gives rise to the nervous system, epidermis, hair, nails and skin glands, the lens, retina and inner ear, the pituitary and adrenal medulla, and tooth enamel.","w":["Mesoderm forms bone, muscle, heart, vessels.","Endoderm forms gut and airway linings.","All are ectodermal.","All are ectodermal."],"k":"Lecturer contact.","rn":[["","Which special senses structures come from ectoderm?","Which special senses structures come from ectoderm?","Lens; Retina; Inner ear"],["","What are the 3 embryonic germ layers?","What are the 3 embryonic germ layers?","Ectoderm; Mesoderm; Endoderm"],["","Which parts of the integumentary system come from ectoderm?","Which parts of the integumentary system come from ectoderm?","Epidermis; Hair; Nails; Skin glands"]]},{"id":17121,"t":"Cranial Cavity","s":"A 22-year-old man is hit on the side of the head by a cricket ball. He seems fine at first but becomes drowsy a few hours later. CT shows a collection of blood between the skull and the outermost meningeal layer. The surgeon names the artery that runs in this plane as the likely source.<br><br>Which artery has bled, and what type of bleed is this?","o":["Middle meningeal artery; extradural haematoma","Middle meningeal artery; subarachnoid haemorrhage into the CSF","A vessel in the subarachnoid space; extradural haematoma","Vessels in the pia mater; bleeding into the venous sinuses between the dura's two layers","The venous sinuses; subdural haematoma under the pia"],"e":"Step 1: blood between the skull and the dura mater lies outside the dura, i.e. extradural. Step 2: the middle meningeal artery runs between the periosteal (outer) layer of the dura and the cranial bones, so bleeding from it, often after a blow to the side of the head, causes an extradural haematoma.","w":["Subarachnoid bleeding is beneath the arachnoid, in the CSF, not between skull and dura.","Subarachnoid vessels lie deep to the arachnoid, not between skull and dura.","The pia closely adheres to the brain, far from the skull.","The blood is between skull and dura, not under the pia."],"k":"Middle meningeal artery between dura and skull → extradural haematoma.","rn":[["","Extradural Haematoma","Extradural Haematoma","Bleeding outside the dura; From the middle meningeal artery; Peels dura off the skull"]]},{"id":17122,"t":"Serous Membranes","s":"A 30-year-old woman with early appendicitis first feels a vague, poorly localised ache around the middle of her abdomen. Hours later, the inflamed appendix touches the lining of the abdominal wall, and she now has sharp pain she can point to in the right lower quadrant.<br><br>Which layers explain the two kinds of pain?","o":["First visceral peritoneum (visceral sensory); later parietal peritoneum (somatic sensory)","First parietal peritoneum (somatic sensory); later visceral peritoneum (visceral sensory)","Both from the visceral layer, which is always sharp","Both from the parietal layer, which is supplied by the visceral sensory nerves","First the pleura; later the pericardium"],"e":"Step 1: the visceral layer covering an organ has visceral sensory innervation, so its pain is usually poorly localised or referred (vague). Step 2: the parietal layer lining the body wall has somatic sensory innervation, so its pain is sharp and well localised; when the inflamed appendix irritates the parietal peritoneum, she can point to the pain.","w":["The order is reversed.","Visceral pain is vague, not sharp.","The parietal layer is somatic sensory.","The pleura and pericardium are thoracic."],"k":"Visceral layer → vague pain; parietal layer → sharp, localised pain.","rn":[["","Visceral vs parietal layer: innervation","Visceral vs parietal layer: innervation","Visceral: visceral sensory; Parietal: somatic sensory"],["","Visceral vs parietal layer: pain","Visceral vs parietal layer: pain","Visceral: vague, referred; Parietal: sharp, localised"]]},{"id":17123,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"Three patients have abnormal collections in the pleural cavity. Patient 1 has a stab wound and a chest drain removes blood. Patient 2 has a chest infection and the fluid drained is pus. Patient 3 had chest surgery that damaged the thoracic duct, and the fluid is milky.<br><br>Which terms describe the three collections?","o":["1: haemothorax; 2: empyema; 3: chylothorax","1: hydrothorax; 2: pneumothorax; 3: haemothorax","1: haemothorax; 2: hydropneumothorax; 3: hydrothorax","1: empyema; 2: haemothorax; 3: chylothorax","All three: pneumothorax, as any abnormal collection in the pleural space has this name"],"e":"Step 1: blood in the pleural cavity is a haemothorax and pus is an empyema (pyothorax). Step 2: chyle (milky lymph, carried by the thoracic duct) is a chylothorax; the general term for abnormal fluid between the visceral and parietal pleura is a pleural effusion.","w":["Hydrothorax is serous fluid and pneumothorax is air.","Pus is empyema, and milky fluid is chyle.","Blood is haemothorax and pus is empyema.","Pneumothorax is air only."],"k":"Blood: haemothorax; pus: empyema; chyle: chylothorax; air: pneumothorax.","rn":[["","Which 3 pleural collections involve blood, chyle or pus?","Which 3 pleural collections involve blood, chyle or pus?","Haemothorax; Chylothorax; Empyema (pyothorax)"]]},{"id":17124,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"A 50-year-old man is in a car crash. CT shows blood collecting behind the peritoneum on the back wall of the abdomen, with injuries to organs that sit directly on the posterior wall and are covered by peritoneum only on their front surface. The organs hanging freely into the peritoneal cavity are uninjured.<br><br>Which organs are most likely injured?","o":["Kidneys and pancreas (retroperitoneal)","Stomach and spleen (intraperitoneal)","Jejunum and ileum (on a mesentery)","Transverse and sigmoid colon (intraperitoneal)","Liver and gallbladder, which are wrapped completely by visceral peritoneum and hang on a mesentery"],"e":"Step 1: retroperitoneal organs lie behind the peritoneum, sitting directly on the posterior wall with visceral peritoneum covering their anterior surface only. Step 2: examples are the kidneys, ureters, adrenal glands, pancreas and ascending and descending colon; intraperitoneal organs such as the stomach, jejunum, ileum, transverse and sigmoid colon and spleen hang on a mesentery.","w":["The stomach and spleen are intraperitoneal.","The jejunum and ileum are on a mesentery.","The transverse and sigmoid colon are intraperitoneal.","These organs are not on the lecture's retroperitoneal list."],"k":"Retroperitoneal: kidneys, ureters, adrenals, pancreas, ascending/descending colon.","rn":[["","Which 3 gut organs are retroperitoneal?","Which 3 gut organs are retroperitoneal?","Pancreas; Ascending colon; Descending colon"],["","Which 3 urinary/endocrine organs are retroperitoneal?","Which 3 urinary/endocrine organs are retroperitoneal?","Kidneys; Ureters; Adrenal glands"]]},{"id":17125,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"A medical student looks at an axial CT of the upper abdomen. The liver appears on the left side of the screen and the spleen on the right side. The vertebra is at the bottom of the image. She wonders whether the scan has been flipped.<br><br>Which explanation is correct?","o":["Axial images are viewed from the feet, so the patient's right is on the viewer's left","The scan is flipped; the liver should be on the right of the screen","Axial images are viewed from the head, so right is right","The vertebra is anterior, so the image is upside down","The liver is on the patient's left side in most people, so the image is correct as shown"],"e":"Step 1: axial CT and sections are viewed as if looking up from the patient's feet, so the patient's right appears on the viewer's left. Step 2: using landmarks, the vertebra is posterior (bottom of the image) and the liver is on the patient's right, so the liver on the screen's left is normal.","w":["The image is correctly displayed.","Axial images are viewed from below (the feet).","The vertebra is posterior.","The liver is on the patient's right."],"k":"Axial images viewed from the feet: patient's right on your left.","rn":[["","Image card: Which side of this axial CT is the patient's right? How can you tell?","Which side of this axial CT is the patient's right? How can you tell?","Screen left; Liver is on right; CT viewed from below"],["","Because CTs are viewed from below, the patient's left is on which side of the screen?","Because CTs are viewed from below, the patient's left is on which side of the screen?","The viewer's right"],["","Image card: Which side of this axial CT is the patient's left? How can you tell?","Which side of this axial CT is the patient's left? How can you tell?","Screen right; Heart tilts to the left; CT is viewed from below"]]},{"id":17126,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"A 60-year-old woman has a tumour in the central compartment of her chest, between the two pleural cavities. It compresses a nerve that supplies her diaphragm, and she also has difficulty swallowing. Her lungs themselves are normal.<br><br>Which compartment is the tumour in, and which structures are affected?","o":["Mediastinum; the phrenic nerve and oesophagus","Right pleural cavity; the right lung and vagus nerve","Pericardial cavity; the heart and aorta","Mediastinum; the thoracic duct and spleen","Peritoneal cavity; the stomach and the subcostal plane"],"e":"Step 1: the mediastinum is everything that lies between the two pleural cavities, including the pericardium and heart, trachea, oesophagus, great vessels, vagus and phrenic nerves and thoracic duct. Step 2: compression of the phrenic nerve (which supplies the diaphragm) and of the oesophagus (difficulty swallowing) fits a mediastinal tumour.","w":["Her lungs are normal, and the pleural cavity holds the lung.","The pericardial cavity is a space around the heart.","The spleen is in the abdomen.","The peritoneal cavity is in the abdomen."],"k":"Mediastinum: between pleural cavities; heart, trachea, oesophagus, vessels, vagus, phrenic.","rn":[["","What are the 3 nerves and lymphatics of the mediastinum?","What are the 3 nerves and lymphatics of the mediastinum?","Vagus nerve; Phrenic nerve; Thoracic duct"]]},{"id":17127,"t":"Abdominal Cavity","s":"A 45-year-old man has pain just below his right ribs, in the region lateral to the right midclavicular plane and above the subcostal plane. A second patient has pain in the central region just above the umbilical region, between the two midclavicular planes.<br><br>Which of the nine regions are these?","o":["Right hypochondrium; epigastric","Right lumbar; umbilical","Right iliac; pubic","Epigastric; left hypochondrium","Right upper quadrant; left upper quadrant, which are two of the nine regions"],"e":"Step 1: the nine regions are formed by the two midclavicular (vertical) planes and the subcostal and intertubercular (horizontal) planes; the top row is the right hypochondrium, epigastric and left hypochondrium. Step 2: pain under the right ribs, lateral to the right midclavicular plane and above the subcostal plane, is the right hypochondrium, and the central top region is the epigastric.","w":["The lumbar and umbilical regions are in the middle row.","The iliac and pubic regions are in the bottom row.","The first pain is on the right, not central.","Quadrants belong to the four-quadrant system, not the nine regions."],"k":"Top row: right hypochondrium, epigastric, left hypochondrium.","rn":[["","What are the 3 upper regions of the abdomen?","What are the 3 upper regions of the abdomen?","Right hypochondrium; Epigastric; Left hypochondrium"]]},{"id":17128,"t":"Thoracic Cavity","s":"A 70-year-old man has inflammation that dries up the pericardial fluid. With every heartbeat, the two layers of his pericardium rub together painfully. His doctor explains what the fluid normally does and how the pericardium differs from the pleura.<br><br>Which statement is correct?","o":["The fluid reduces friction; the pericardium also has an extra fibrous layer","The fluid conducts nerves; the pleura has a fibrous layer the pericardium lacks","The fluid compartmentalises the abdomen; both have three serous layers","The fluid supports the heart's weight; the pericardium has no parietal layer","The fluid defends against infection only; the pleura and pericardium are identical in every way"],"e":"Step 1: the space between the visceral and parietal layers of a serous membrane is filled with fluid, and its first function is to reduce friction. Step 2: the pleura has visceral and parietal layers, while the pericardium has visceral and parietal serous layers plus a fibrous outer layer.","w":["The fluid reduces friction, and only the pericardium has a fibrous layer.","Both have two serous layers; only the pericardium adds a fibrous layer.","The pericardium has a parietal layer.","The pericardium differs by having a fibrous layer."],"k":"Serous fluid reduces friction; pericardium adds a fibrous layer.","rn":[["","What is the one exception that makes the pericardium different from the pleura?","What is the one exception that makes the pericardium different from the pleura?","An extra outer fibrous layer"],["","How do serous membranes reduce friction?","How do serous membranes reduce friction?","Fluid fills the space; Between the layers"],["","Fibrous pericardium","Fibrous pericardium","Fibrous layer of the pericardium"]]},{"id":17129,"t":"Cranial Cavity","s":"A doctor performs a lumbar puncture on a 25-year-old with suspected meningitis. The needle passes through the tough outer meningeal layer, then the web-like middle layer, and clear fluid is collected from the space beneath. The innermost layer, stuck to the cord's surface, is not entered.<br><br>Which space has the fluid come from, and what does it contain?","o":["Subarachnoid space, between arachnoid and pia; CSF and blood vessels","Extradural space, between dura and bone; venous sinuses","Between the two layers of dura; CSF","Pleural space; serous fluid","Subdural space between the pia and the cord; the middle meningeal artery"],"e":"Step 1: the meninges from outside in are the dura mater, arachnoid mater and pia mater; the pia closely adheres to the surface of the brain and spinal cord. Step 2: the arachnoid is separated from the pia by the subarachnoid space, which contains cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) and blood vessels.","w":["The needle passed through the dura and arachnoid into a deeper space.","Venous sinuses lie between the two layers of cranial dura, not CSF.","The pleural space is in the thorax.","The pia is stuck to the cord, and the middle meningeal artery is cranial and extradural."],"k":"Subarachnoid space (arachnoid–pia) contains CSF and vessels.","rn":[["","Subarachnoid Space","Subarachnoid Space","Space between arachnoid and pia; Contains CSF and blood vessels"]]},{"id":17130,"t":"Embryonic Germ Layer Derivatives","s":"A baby has a developmental defect affecting tissues from one germ layer. The adrenal medulla, epidermis and lens are abnormal, while the adrenal cortex, dermis and kidneys are normal. The bladder lining is also normal.<br><br>Which germ layer is affected, and which layer forms the spared tissues?","o":["Ectoderm; the cortex, dermis and kidneys are mesoderm, and the bladder lining is endoderm","Mesoderm; the cortex, dermis and kidneys are ectoderm","Endoderm; the cortex and dermis are endoderm too","Ectoderm; the cortex, dermis, kidneys and bladder lining are all ectoderm","Mesoderm; the adrenal medulla and lens are mesoderm, while the epidermis is endoderm"],"e":"Step 1: the ectoderm forms the nervous system, epidermis, lens and adrenal medulla, so these are affected. Step 2: the adrenal cortex, dermis and kidneys come from mesoderm, while the bladder lining comes from endoderm, so they are spared.","w":["The affected tissues are ectoderm, and the spared ones are mesoderm.","The affected tissues are ectodermal, not endodermal.","The spared tissues come from mesoderm and endoderm.","The adrenal medulla, lens and epidermis are ectoderm."],"k":"Adrenal medulla/epidermis = ectoderm; cortex/dermis/kidney = mesoderm; bladder lining = endoderm.","rn":[["","Kidneys/ureters vs bladder/urethra lining: germ layer","Kidneys/ureters vs bladder/urethra lining: germ layer","Kidneys, ureters: mesoderm; Bladder, urethra lining: endoderm"],["","Which 2 germ layers give urinary structures?","Which 2 germ layers give urinary structures?","Mesoderm (kidneys, ureters); Endoderm (bladder, urethra lining)"],["","Adrenal medulla vs adrenal cortex: germ layer","Adrenal medulla vs adrenal cortex: germ layer","Medulla: ectoderm; Cortex: mesoderm"]]},{"id":17501,"t":"Body Cavities and Their Linings","s":"A patient has meningitis. Name the layers lining cavity <b>A</b> that are inflamed.","a":["meninges","meningeal layers","meninx"],"e":"A is the cranial cavity, lined by the meninges (dura, arachnoid, pia); meningitis inflames the subarachnoid space between arachnoid and pia.","k":"A = cranial cavity, meninges.","img":{"src":"17/o_cavities","cap":"Body cavities, lateral view. Image: public domain, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Cranial cavity vs other cavities: lining","Cranial cavity vs other cavities: lining","Cranial: meninges; Others: serous membrane"],["","Cranial cavity","Cranial cavity","Skull part holding the brain; Lined by meninges"]]},{"id":17502,"t":"Body Cavities and Their Linings","s":"Blood collects in the serous space around the lungs within cavity <b>C</b>. Name the serous membrane lining this space.","a":["pleura","pleurae","parietal pleura","visceral pleura"],"e":"C is the thoracic cavity; the lungs are wrapped by pleura. Blood there is a haemothorax.","k":"C = thoracic, pleura.","img":{"src":"17/o_cavities","cap":"Body cavities, lateral view. Image: public domain, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Pleura","Pleura","Lung"],["","Which membranes are in the thoracic cavity?","Which membranes are in the thoracic cavity?","Pleura; Pericardium"],["","What are the 2 layers of the pleura?","What are the 2 layers of the pleura?","Visceral pleura; Parietal pleura"]]},{"id":17503,"t":"Body Cavities and Their Linings","s":"A stab wound enters cavity <b>D</b> below the diaphragm. Name the serous membrane lining it.","a":["peritoneum","parietal peritoneum"],"e":"D is the abdominal cavity, lined by peritoneum; its inflammation gives sharp, well-localised pain (somatic supply to the parietal layer).","k":"D = abdominal, peritoneum.","img":{"src":"17/o_cavities","cap":"Body cavities, lateral view. Image: public domain, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Peritoneum","Peritoneum","Abdominal cavity and its organs"],["","What does the parietal peritoneum do?","What does the parietal peritoneum do?","Lines the abdominal cavity (wall)"]]},{"id":17504,"t":"Serous Membranes","s":"Which labelling of B, C, D and E is correct?","o":["B spinal; C thoracic; D abdominal; E pelvic","B spinal; C abdominal; D thoracic; E pelvic","B thoracic; C spinal; D abdominal; E pelvic","B spinal; C thoracic; D pelvic; E abdominal","B cranial; C thoracic; D abdominal; E pelvic"],"w":["C lies above the diaphragm: thoracic.","B is in the vertebral column: spinal.","E is the lowest: pelvic.","The cranial cavity is A."],"e":"Dorsal cavity: cranial (A) and spinal (B). Ventral cavity: thoracic (C) above the diaphragm, and abdominopelvic: abdominal (D) and pelvic (E).","k":"Dorsal: cranial + spinal; ventral: thoracic + abdominopelvic.","img":{"src":"17/o_cavities","cap":"Body cavities, lateral view. Image: public domain, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Which body walls can the parietal layer line?","Which body walls can the parietal layer line?","Thoracic wall; Abdominal wall"]]}]);
